{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2485 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "bdfa13f97f745416c1c020fe0e546faefd6f768643b4784131f29c5d13eb96f7" :edb/db-before-t 2484 :edb/db-after-t 2485 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535797 50 #inst "2026-10-14T23:11:02.344Z" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186052277 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052278 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186052277 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052279 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186052277 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052280 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186052277 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052281 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186052277 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052282 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186052277 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528492 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186052277 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528495 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186052277 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528628 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186052277 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528790 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186052277 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528802 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186052277 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528628 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054486 1074 "The vertex is the \"turning point\" of the parabola. In a downwards parabola, like the one here, the vertex is the maximum point. So, the vertex of this parabola is $(-2,3)$.\n\nThe axis of symmetry is the vertical line that passes through the vertex. Therefore, the axis of symmetry of this parabola is $x=-2$.\n\n![](images/91/91e9d323945b1520aa36b70f3faf8c801e28b21437cd1008b54e76dae76d5ccb.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186054486 1074 "The vertex is the \"turning point\" of the parabola. In a downwards parabola, like the one here, the vertex is the maximum point. So, the vertex of this parabola is $(-2,3).$\n\nThe axis of symmetry is the vertical line that passes through the vertex. Therefore, the axis of symmetry of this parabola is $x=-2.$\n\n![](images/91/91e9d323945b1520aa36b70f3faf8c801e28b21437cd1008b54e76dae76d5ccb.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054487 1074 "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-2,0)$ and $(4,0)$.\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-2$ and $x=4$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186054487 1074 "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola. \n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-2,0)$ and $(4,0).$ \n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-2$ and $x=4.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054488 1071 "Sketch the graph of $y=-x^2+4x-3$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186054488 1071 "Sketch the graph of $y=-x^2+4x-3.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054488 1074 "Let's construct a table of values for the function\n\n$$\ny=-x^2+4x-3\n$$\n\nWe fill the first row with the $x$-values $x=0,1,2,3,4$ and then use the formula\n\n$$\ny=-x^2+4x-3\n$$\n\nto compute the corresponding $y$-values in the second row.\n\n| $\\,\\,x\\,\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\,\\,y\\,\\,$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-3$ |\n\nIf we plot those points on a graph, we can start to see the shape of a parabola:\n\n![](images/44/442d3550eb77f837da6875bc3877023db44f9f3ee862b433fa56d2186c352bd2.png)\n\nWe fill out the graph by drawing the parabola through the plotted points:\n\n![](images/e9/e95ebb53e7924dc1e99a92be85e6981def3c1186029c6c620bded4bb797f0c06.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186054488 1074 "Let's construct a table of values for the function $y=-x^2+4x-3.$ We fill the first row with the $x$-values $x=0,1,2,3,4$ and then use the formula $y=-x^2+4x-3$ to compute the corresponding $y$-values in the second row.\n\n| $\\,\\,x\\,\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\,y\\,\\,$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-3$ |\n\nIf we plot those points on a graph, we can start to see the shape of a parabola:\n\n![](images/44/442d3550eb77f837da6875bc3877023db44f9f3ee862b433fa56d2186c352bd2.png)\n\nWe fill out the graph by drawing the parabola through the plotted points:\n\n![](images/e9/e95ebb53e7924dc1e99a92be85e6981def3c1186029c6c620bded4bb797f0c06.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054578 1071 "![](images/9e/9e012ecda75df732e1c96ea4a52762fb085f444f41991ed9a3fe72d7d8e10025.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=x^2+2x+k$. Find the value of $k$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186054578 1071 "![](images/9e/9e012ecda75df732e1c96ea4a52762fb085f444f41991ed9a3fe72d7d8e10025.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=x^2+2x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054578 1074 "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{2} + 2x + k \\\\\ny &= (0)^{2} + 2(0) + k \\\\\ny &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k)$. Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=0$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at\n\n$$\n\\left(0,0\\right)\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186054578 1074 "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}+2x+k \\\\ y & =(0)^{2}+2(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=0$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $\\left(0,0\\right).$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529091 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529127 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054721 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529097 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054721 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529151 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054721 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529164 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054721 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529178 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529123 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529126 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186054926 1050 420.0 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186055158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186055158 1074 "The vertex is the \"turning point\" of the parabola. In an upwards parabola, like the one here, the vertex is the minimum point. So, the vertex of this parabola is $(3,-2).$\n\nThe axis of symmetry is the vertical line that passes through the vertex. Therefore, the axis of symmetry of this parabola is $x=3.$\n\n![](images/f1/f139192260875d5e988f30a64a7024f50d68337dbd4969d938f18aa62b850dd0.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186055952 1064 "Consider the graph of the parabola ![](images/f2/f218c572691a74963cd3808bf4df2923122e84b65d1fabf1138bab6264b043b5.svg) shown below.\n\n![](images/45/450251899f728a3cc927b72fdb7655204828c8e0be0432cb08cee2462d6e5bfe.png)\n\nWe can describe many features of the graph using mathematical terminology:\n\n- The ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-intercepts of the parabola are at ![](images/81/8171bb8403bd612f56ef5f32f400f1aef8efeed51ea279d36779771fdea1ae0d.svg) and ![](images/77/779cec712506e0f798805963ab85841b46650fb22681d97a64aec4b85a47d07f.svg) We say that ![](images/b0/b064281df4bef2506521322c7477468b9cc565616e2c5b6f21000df6522399c1.svg) and ![](images/1f/1fb9f2f73a09599931892c1dee7629975b4d851e5dd6f0d07b7c641bbf17bf54.svg) are the **roots** (or **zeros**) of the parabola.\n\n- The parabola intersects the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis at  ![](images/6a/6a0e2cbf9f56c3c989ad96f020fe8bb0f7c785ff6de8073c476232b95476ac13.svg). We say that ![](images/78/78d4465d85b55cee3832a9b598a2564b6a3fa9a072de8a1fb4b40ca884079670.svg) is the **![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept**. The ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept is the same as the constant term of the function.\n\n- The parabola has a \"turning point\" at  ![](images/ef/ef43561f9d825c9e79fb03dffafa744d386d0c2a5a07da903675bca120d59fd1.svg) which is called the **vertex**. In an upwards parabola (like the one shown above), the vertex is the minimum point, while in a downwards parabola, the vertex is the maximum point.\n\n- The vertical line  ![](images/df/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg) that passes through the vertex is called the **axis of symmetry**. The part of the parabola to the left of the axis of symmetry is the mirror image of the right, and vice versa.\n\n- This particular parabola is **upward opening** because it has a distinctive ![](images/13/13ab0b72e7963f3e74404b8b75f12654ece94c2c44e1fb09fdf230b976eb681b.svg) shape. A parabola ![](images/7c/7c49eede8f0eb80dadd8384ae942dc8248ef454eeb6afa1aeba7ae48e7545456.svg) is upward opening if the quadratic coefficient is positive ![](images/d2/d2a0fc4d90b45e06999c89b1a40ff6f01116e9c429c195adbfced573f9e47660.svg)\n\n- Parabolas can also be **downward opening** with a distinctive ![](images/57/57872695daf3067460adec5a506cfb1457729de4eeac879eab9f7355570bac11.svg) shape. A parabola ![](images/7c/7c49eede8f0eb80dadd8384ae942dc8248ef454eeb6afa1aeba7ae48e7545456.svg) is downward opening if the quadratic coefficient is negative ![](images/a1/a16d199f292dbd74941402b3d485cf107a7a985d28aeb13ab50ff6baf87f2d4b.svg)\n\nLet's take a look at an example of a downward opening parabola." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186055952 1064 "Consider the graph of the parabola $y=x^2-2x-3$, shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c560a02b9c499128b6911aec66ad8ecf1cee2ee3c7d607b80b6e6962fc441289.png)\n\nWe can describe many features of the graph using mathematical terminology:\n\n- The $x$-intercepts of the parabola are at $(-1,0)$ and $(3,0)$. We say that $x=-1$ and $x=3$ are the **roots** (or **zeros**) of the parabola.\n- The parabola intersects the $y$-axis at $\\color{red}(0,-3)$. We say that $-3$ is the **$y$-intercept**. The $y$-intercept is the same as the constant term of the function.\n- The parabola has a \"turning point\" at $\\color{blue}(1,-4)$ which is called the **vertex**. In an upwards parabola (like the one shown above), the vertex is the minimum point, while in a downwards parabola, the vertex is the maximum point.\n- The vertical line $\\color{blue}x=1$ that passes through the vertex is called the **axis of symmetry**. The part of the parabola to the left of the axis of symmetry is the mirror image of the right, and vice versa.\n- This particular parabola is **upward opening** because it has a distinctive $\\smile$ shape. A parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is upward opening if the quadratic coefficient is positive $(a > 0)$.\n- Parabolas can also be **downward opening** with a distinctive $\\frown$ shape. A parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is downward opening if the quadratic coefficient is negative $(a < 0)$.\n\nLet's take a look at an example of a downward opening parabola." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065518 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065519 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065520 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065521 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065522 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528451 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528554 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528670 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528769 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528864 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065517 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528554 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065523 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065524 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065523 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065525 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065523 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065527 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065523 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065528 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065523 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528327 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065523 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528552 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065523 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528742 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065523 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528777 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065523 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528777 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065529 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065530 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065529 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065531 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065529 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065532 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065529 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065533 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065529 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065534 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186065529 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528423 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065529 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528468 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065529 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528526 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065529 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528679 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065529 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528688 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186065529 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528526 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186066075 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066076 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186066075 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066077 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186066075 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066078 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186066075 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066079 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186066075 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066080 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186066075 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528320 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186066075 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528420 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186066075 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528529 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186066075 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528696 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186066075 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528935 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186066075 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528320 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186066736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066737 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186066736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066738 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186066736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066739 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186066736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066740 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186066736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066741 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186066736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528318 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186066736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528329 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186066736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528361 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186066736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528446 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186066736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528606 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186066736 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528318 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068503 1071 "Identify the equation of the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/36/36a2a45f6078cdf04b798c39b6382c1f40411375a3f16ef77e996b39aa0009b3.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068503 1071 "Identify the equation of the graph shown below. \n\n![](images/36/36a2a45f6078cdf04b798c39b6382c1f40411375a3f16ef77e996b39aa0009b3.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068503 1074 "Remember that the graph of\n\n$$\ny=(x-h)^2+k\n$$\n\nis a translation of the standard parabola $y=x^2$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of\n\n$$\ny=(x-h)^2+k\n$$\n\nis located at $(h,k)$.\n\nHere, the parabola is translated $2$ units *left*, so we have $h=-2$. The parabola is also translated $1$ unit up, so we have $k=1$. We can also see that the vertex is at $(-2,1)$, so again we must have $h=-2$ and $k=1$.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the graph is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (x - (-2))^{2} + 1 \\\\\ny &= (x + 2)^{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068503 1074 "Remember that the graph of $y=(x-h)^2+k$ is a translation of the standard parabola $y=x^2$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of $y=(x-h)^2+k$ is located at $(h,k).$\n\nHere, the parabola is translated $2$ units *left*, so we have $h=-2.$ The parabola is also translated $1$ unit up, so we have $k=1.$ We can also see that the vertex is at $(-2,1),$ so again we must have $h=-2$ and $k=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the graph is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x-(-2))^{2}+1 \\\\ y & =(x+2)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068525 1074 "Remember that in general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=x^2+k\n$$\n\nlooks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $k$ units up. The vertex of\n\n$$\ny=x^2+k\n$$\n\nis located at $(0,k)$.\n\nHere, the curve shown is the parabola $y=x^2$ after being translated $3$ units upward. The vertex is located at $(0,3)$.\n\nTherefore, $k=3$, and the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\ny = x^2 +3\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068525 1074 "Remember that in general, the graph of $y=x^2+k$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $k$ units up. The vertex of $y=x^2+k$ is located at $(0,k).$\n\nHere, the curve shown is the parabola $y=x^2$ after being translated $3$ units upward. The vertex is located at $(0,3).$\n\nTherefore, $k=3,$ and the equation of the parabola is $y = x^2 +3.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068526 1071 "Sketch the graph of the function $y=(x+3)^2$. What are the coordinates of the minimum point?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068526 1071 "Sketch the graph of the function $y=(x+3)^2.$ What are the coordinates of the minimum point?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068526 1074 "In general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^2$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^2$ is located at $(h,0)$.\n\nIn the given function\n\n$$\ny=(x+3)^2\n$$\n\nwe have $h=-3$. To see this clearly, we can write the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (x + 3)^{2} \\\\\ny &= (x - (-3))^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y=x^2$ after being translated $3$ units *left*.\n\n![](images/bf/bffdacd979eaa77a7a64364840ab318bcc4a1c39ca78137146e82f37540e02e4.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(-3,0)$, and these are the coordinates of the minimum point." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068526 1074 "In general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^2$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^2$ is located at $(h,0).$\n\nIn the given function $y=(x+3)^2,$ we have $h=-3.$ To see this clearly, we can write the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x+3)^{2} \\\\ y & =(x-(-3))^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y=x^2$ after being translated $3$ units *left*. \n\n![](images/bf/bffdacd979eaa77a7a64364840ab318bcc4a1c39ca78137146e82f37540e02e4.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(-3,0),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068617 1071 "![](images/73/7373418f2a0bac416e37662cea5a79101220820aa6f23c50a80fbf6addc5eefd.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=x^2$ and $y=x^5$, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068617 1071 "![](images/73/7373418f2a0bac416e37662cea5a79101220820aa6f23c50a80fbf6addc5eefd.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=x^2$ and $y=x^5,$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068617 1074 "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nand $g(x)=x^5$.\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\nx^{2} &= x^{5} \\\\\nx^{5} - x^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}(x^{3} - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$.\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/e4/e41991c460504023c4086aa0070904f20d88304aecd42a293c4b0422132b40e0.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=x^5$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=x^2$.\n\nTherefore, the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{a}^{b}([outer]^{2} - [inner]^{2})dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}([x^{2}]^{2} - [x^{5}]^{2})dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}(x^{4} - x^{10})dx \\\\\n&= \\left.π(\\frac{x^{5}}{5} - \\frac{x^{11}}{11})\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= π[(\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{11}) - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{6π}{55}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068617 1074 "Let $f(x)=x^2$ and $g(x)=x^5.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={x}^{5} \\\\ {x}^{5}-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{3}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/e4/e41991c460504023c4086aa0070904f20d88304aecd42a293c4b0422132b40e0.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=x^5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=x^2.$\n\nTherefore, the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}({[\\text{outer}]}^{2}-{[\\text{inner}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[{x}^{5}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-{x}^{10})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{11})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi }{55}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068618 1071 "![](images/cd/cd7319762569fbab0e64f03719b41af9dfb45d07aac073a534ea68f842b1ce99.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $x=\\sqrt{4-y^2}$, $x=1$, and the horizontal lines $y=-1$ and $y=1$, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068618 1071 "![](images/cd/cd7319762569fbab0e64f03719b41af9dfb45d07aac073a534ea68f842b1ce99.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $x=\\sqrt{4-y^2},$ $x=1,$ and the horizontal lines $y=-1$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068618 1074 "Let\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\sqrt{4-y^2}\\,\n$$\n\nand $g(y)=1$.\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7f/7f875c024b25c37c434a82a4320b4927bde85dc8f0ada3b35837330f829b23c7.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=1$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{4-y^2}$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{c}^{d}([outer]^{2} - [inner]^{2})dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{1}([\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}]^{2} - 1^{2})dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{1}(3 - y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}(3 - y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= \\left.2π(3y - \\frac{y^{3}}{3})\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 2π(3 - \\frac{1}{3} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{16π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068618 1074 "Let $f(y)=\\sqrt{4-y^2}\\,$ and $g(y)=1.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7f/7f875c024b25c37c434a82a4320b4927bde85dc8f0ada3b35837330f829b23c7.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{4-y^2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}]}^{2}-{1}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (3y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (3-\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068645 1071 "![](images/f6/f6ee4ef4870da694c0ef56cf7395012c9ea1edbe4bc31247d9cdc7b26186b7db.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and $y=x^3$, as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068645 1071 "![](images/f6/f6ee4ef4870da694c0ef56cf7395012c9ea1edbe4bc31247d9cdc7b26186b7db.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and $y=x^3,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068645 1074 "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nand $g(x)=x^3$.\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\n\\sqrt{x} &= x^{3} \\\\\nx &= x^{6} \\\\\nx^{6} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{5} - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$.\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/2a/2abe3a274ab5afee2bc0a85c48c2f1218620b86354c3c4e6e4dd8758ca1af5e5.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=x^3$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{a}^{b}([outer]^{2} - [inner]^{2})dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}([\\sqrt{x}]^{2} - [x^{3}]^{2})dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}(x - x^{6})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068645 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)=x^3.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & ={x}^{6} \\\\ {x}^{6}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{5}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/2a/2abe3a274ab5afee2bc0a85c48c2f1218620b86354c3c4e6e4dd8758ca1af5e5.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=x^3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}-{[{x}^{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068646 1071 "![](images/97/97ead66fca615e8f9325d445ca14a8478a276e6dc8d2878a2fd5dbea5d880f7a.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=x^3$ and $x=y$, where $y \\leq 0$, as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068646 1071 "![](images/97/97ead66fca615e8f9325d445ca14a8478a276e6dc8d2878a2fd5dbea5d880f7a.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=x^3$ and $x=y,$ where $y \\leq 0,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068646 1074 "Writing our first function in terms of $y$, we get\n\n$$\ny = x^{3}⟹x = \\sqrt[3]{y}\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}\n$$\n\nand $g(y)=y$.\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y) &= g(y) \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{y} &= y \\\\\ny &= y^{3} \\\\\ny^{3} - y &= 0 \\\\\ny(y^{2} - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $y=0$ and $y=\\pm 1$. But, since\n\n$$\ny \\leq 0\n$$\n\nthe limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=-1$.\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/1a/1ab4ce88964c1a4ce12fdfb93b77810b675bc18e656060629c3544eea47129b2.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=y$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{a}^{b}([outer]^{2} - [inner]^{2})dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{0}([\\sqrt[3]{y}]^{2} - [y]^{2})dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{0}(\\sqrt[3]{y^{2}} - y^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186068646 1074 "Writing our first function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =y \\\\ y & ={y}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $y=0$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ But, since $y \\leq 0,$ the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=-1.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/1a/1ab4ce88964c1a4ce12fdfb93b77810b675bc18e656060629c3544eea47129b2.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[\\sqrt[3]{y}]}^{2}-{[y]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186068775 1038 #edb/ref 1