erated when this region is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069015 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $x=\\sqrt{y}$ and $x={y}^{3}.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069015 1074 "Let $f(y)=\\sqrt{y}$ and $g(y)={y}^{3}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & ={y}^{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{y}-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{y}\\,(1-{y}^{5/2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/cd/cdf95299f8b925adcec743141bda4d94ad5542bfa8859fbdf5501ae0d56cd4e8.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)={y}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}-{[{y}^{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{6})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{7}}{7})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{7})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069016 1071 "![](images/62/6250d388875796ad05e4d3eb8a5f625b6a633cbf0e9efb24926f72a9c6ceac7f.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y = 1 - x^{2}$ and $x = 1 - \\sqrt{y}$, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069016 1071 "![](images/62/6250d388875796ad05e4d3eb8a5f625b6a633cbf0e9efb24926f72a9c6ceac7f.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $x=1-\\sqrt{y},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069016 1074 "Writing our first function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)=\\sqrt{1-y}\\,$ and $g(y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt{1-y} & =1-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ 1-y & =1-2\\sqrt{y}+y \\\\ 2y-2\\sqrt{y} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{y}(\\sqrt{y}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/b6/b630edf4ad0b61defcb7aba67902f75202a8f6e9a209384fb28650c06545113f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=1-\\sqrt{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{1-y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{1-y}]}^{2}-{[1-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}([1-y]-[1-2\\sqrt{y}+y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2\\sqrt{y}-2y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{1/2}-y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {(\\frac{2{y}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069107 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $y = 2\\sqrt{x}$ and $y = 2x$. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069107 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=2\\sqrt{x}$ and $y=2x.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069107 1074 "Let $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)=2x.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =2x \\\\ x-\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}(\\sqrt{x}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/d0/d0b8efe1137ecce643a16b690ba1b05f72a8f7f1e90a56e0b6c379d95886f321.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=2x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}-{[2x]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x-4{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(2{x}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(2-\\frac{4}{3})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069261 1071 "![](images/3f/3f27cf1fddfbbbdb9dd2593567743494ae15e061bca8d1e111ac1f1561561943.png)\n\nConsider the finite region above bounded by $y = x^{2}$ and $y = \\sqrt{x}$, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069261 1071 "![](images/3f/3f27cf1fddfbbbdb9dd2593567743494ae15e061bca8d1e111ac1f1561561943.png)\n\nConsider the finite region above bounded by $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069261 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}({x}^{3/2}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/bf/bf8d9039cbaa7fe5559d8d9d036063739e572b2abc46741668a88c09d9bcd28a.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=f(x)={x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=g(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}-{[{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069690 1061 2081 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069690 1061 639680 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069691 1061 2083 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069691 1061 639684 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069692 1061 2087 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069692 1061 639687 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069693 1061 2089 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069693 1061 639691 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069726 1061 27071 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069726 1061 639681 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069727 1061 27072 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069727 1061 639688 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069920 1064 "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nthe curve\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the vertical line $x=2$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/65/651d5d0d9ccafdeb94608e74086136b121c394670afbea669c3e99606f9756aa.png)\n\nWe rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis to get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/7a/7a1e4b8d0a27283cad7a0b0e0f2b23f1a45d0e560582a61f84c0f90a4f61e039.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the outer curve $y=f(x)$, the inner curve $y=g(x)$, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\Big(\\big[ \\underbrace{f(x)}_\\text{outer} \\big]^2 - \\big[ \\underbrace{g(x)}_\\text{inner} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis method of computing the volume is called the **washer method** because it involves breaking up the region into many thin washers (one such washer is shown below).\n\n![](images/29/2920f308f15ef81eaff0ba431d0f4eec3ccd9826fbde8f126242b0493f1f8e32.png)\n\nThe cross-sectional area of each washer corresponds to the region between two discs of radius $f(x)$ and $g(x)$, so the area of each washer cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\pi [f(x)]^2 - \\pi [g(x)]^2\n$$\n\nand this is what we integrate.\n\nIn our case, the outer curve is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nand the inner curve is\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nso the volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = π\\int_{0}^{2}(\\sqrt{x})^{2} - (\\frac{1}{2}x)^{2}dx\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nV = π\\int_{0}^{2}(x - \\frac{x^{2}}{4})dx\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π(\\int_{0}^{2}xdx - \\frac{1}{4}\\int_{0}^{2}x^{2}dx) \\\\\n&= \\left.π(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right|_{0}^{2} - \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\left.\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right|_{0}^{2}) \\\\\n&= π((\\frac{4}{2} - 0) - \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{8}{3} - 0)) \\\\\n&= π(2 - \\frac{2}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the volume of the solid is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\ncubic units." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069920 1064 "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x,$ and the vertical line $x=2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/65/651d5d0d9ccafdeb94608e74086136b121c394670afbea669c3e99606f9756aa.png)\n\nWe rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis to get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/7a/7a1e4b8d0a27283cad7a0b0e0f2b23f1a45d0e560582a61f84c0f90a4f61e039.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the outer curve $y=f(x),$ the inner curve $y=g(x),$ and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\Big( \\big[ \\underbrace{f(x)}_\\text{outer} \\big]^2 - \\big[ \\underbrace{g(x)}_\\text{inner} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method of computing the volume is called the **washer method** because it involves breaking up the region into many thin washers (one such washer is shown below). \n\n![](images/29/2920f308f15ef81eaff0ba431d0f4eec3ccd9826fbde8f126242b0493f1f8e32.png)\n\nThe cross-sectional area of each washer corresponds to the region between two discs of radius $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ so the area of each washer cross-section is \n\n$$\n\\pi [f(x)]^2 - \\pi [g(x)]^2,\n$$\n\nand this is what we integrate.\n\nIn our case, the outer curve is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and the inner curve is $g(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x,$ so the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{2}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi ((\\frac{4}{2}-0)-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{8}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the volume of the solid is $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ cubic units." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069921 1064 "Consider the plane region bounded by the curve\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nthe curve $x=y^2$, and the horizontal line\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/90/908d37e7b02ffb2b585c29bd11e3adfc92b005e0d7b205bcea258461b98d9ee6.png)\n\nWe rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $y$-axis to get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/b6/b675045622be931c60199474e9fe5131ad736f09061746f1d1f966690bd6d5cd.png)\n\nTo find the volume of this solid, we can use the same washer method approach that we used earlier for the $x$-axis. The only difference is that we need to swap $x$ and $y$ in our formula.\n\nSo, to find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the outer curve $x=f(y)$, the inner curve $x=g(y)$, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{c}^{d}π([f(y)_(⏟)_(outer)]^{2} - [g(y)_(⏟)_(inner)]^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the same washer method as before, the only difference is that we need to swap $x$ and $y$. It involves breaking up the region into many thin washers (one such washer is shown below).\n\n![](images/ba/ba94d5e2ac72140fadd80298390d1b0f36b00f46037c8356110e79d4ac6d50dd.png)\n\nThe cross-sectional area of each washer corresponds to the region between two discs of radius $f(y)$ and $g(y)$, so the area of each washer cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\pi [f(y)]^2 - \\pi [g(y)]^2\n$$\n\nand this is what we integrate.\n\nIn our example, the outer curve is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nand the inner curve is $x=y^2$, so the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{0}^{1/2}π([\\sqrt{y}]^{2} - [y^{2}]^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{0}^{1/2}(y - y^{4})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π(\\int_{0}^{1/2}ydy - \\int_{0}^{1/2}y^{4}dy) \\\\\n&= \\left.π(\\frac{y^{2}}{2}\\right|_{0}^{1/2} - \\left.\\frac{y^{5}}{5}\\right|_{0}^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= π((\\frac{1}{8} - 0) - (\\frac{1}{160} - 0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{19π}{160}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069921 1064 "Consider the plane region bounded by the curve ![](images/da/da9e7c622c9bb4816126baaf7030000b0df743977b620a20454f6f41974d4a66.svg) the curve ![](images/34/344c03d67f3618b1dc0560c072fc86443bf8eb991de0eed6ed1196fe20586053.svg) and the horizontal line ![](images/51/5105b7bf189fe285a6ab74d0f01cdea25d5acb40cac53551b0ac58def8d4180f.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c3d071079a6f434b