0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nWe can now plot the graph using the $(x,y)$ values in our table.\n\n![](images/57/576148f61e683e58c508b212221c103d9ef9914f549afabfec92a04ec778a268.png)\n\nThis curve's shape is known as a **parabola**, and the **minimum point** at $(0,0)$ is known as the **vertex** of the parabola." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069952 1064 "Horizontal and vertical translations can be combined. It does not matter which we do first.\n\nIn general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=(x-h)^2+k\n$$\n\nis a translation of the standard parabola $y=x^2$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of\n\n$$\ny=(x-h)^2+k\n$$\n\nis located at $(h,k)$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186069952 1064 "Horizontal and vertical translations can be combined. It does not matter which we do first.\n\nIn general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^2+k$ is a translation of the standard parabola $y=x^2$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of $y=(x-h)^2+k$ is located at $(h,k).$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186069953 1064 "The graph of  ![](images/78/7816c015ff36e92e579c157d0d1e074270678c48b06f0c2d37d920311224d5e5.svg) looks like the standard parabola ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg), but shifted ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) units to the *right* along the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis. \n\n![](images/a4/a48db73aadd4192f244676c3cb54c6849ca284acf1adbf29d6245baa5a07afcb.png)\n\nSimilarly, the graph of ![](images/ce/ce16aa1f1cc35aaeae8aba5c19c0ebf1e9ea43e9624f0c6c18b42f7bfa21c304.svg) looks like the standard ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg) parabola after it's been shifted ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to the *left*:\n\n![](images/9c/9c33ae042e23cee23f96a35f