=3(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2})+4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-4+\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi -8}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186092761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186092761 1071 "Given that $a_{1} =-18$ and $\\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} =-0.9$ for all integers $n \\ge 1$, find the value of $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186092761 1071 "Given that ${a}_{1}=-18$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.9$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186092761 1074 "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-18.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.9.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-18}{1-(-0.9)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{1+0.9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{1.9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{180}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186092790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186092790 1071 "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186092790 1071 "Evaluate the series $∑_(n = 2)^(∞)(\\frac{1}{4})^{n}$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186092790 1074 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}=\\frac{1}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{64})}{(\\frac{1}{16})}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{16})}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{16})}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186093082 1061 2214 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186093082 1061 636917 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186093083 1061 2215 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186093083 1061 636918 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186093084 1061 2219 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186093084 1061 636921 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186093127 1061 25306 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186093127 1061 636924 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186093145 1061 28203 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186093145 1061 636927 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186093357 1064 "Suppose we want to find the area of the region bounded by the polar curve $r = 3\\cos\\theta$ and the rays $\\theta_1 = 0$ and $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as shown in blue in the picture below. Can we calculate this area using polar coordinates?\n\n![](images/2c/2c7e638a338aed2155ef5bf388be1f83a3a67d2296d5be09987b099a07edacbd.png)\n\nIn general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta=\\theta_1$ and $\\theta=\\theta_2$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2 \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the half-circle above can be obtained as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186093357 1064 "Suppose we want to find the area of the region bounded by the polar curve $r = 3\\cos\\theta$ and the rays $\\theta_1 = 0$ and $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as shown in blue in the picture below. Can we calculate this area using polar coordinates?\n\n![](images/2c/2c7e638a338aed2155ef5bf388be1f83a3a67d2296d5be09987b099a07edacbd.png)\n\nIn general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta=\\theta_1$ and $\\theta=\\theta_2$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2 \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the half-circle above can be obtained as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186093368 1064 "Suppose we want to evaluate the infinite geometric series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1} ^ \\infty 3 \\left (\\dfrac 1 4\\right) ^ n\n$$\n\nThe sum $S_\\infty$ of an infinite geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS_{\\infty} = \\dfrac {\\color{black}a_1} {1 - {\\color{black}r}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{black}r}$ is the common ratio and $\\color{black}a_1$ is the first term. So, we need to determine $\\color{black}a_1$ and $\\color{black}r$ and then plug those values into this formula.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}a_1} = 3 \\left(\\dfrac 1 4 \\right)^1 = {\\color{black}\\dfrac 3 4}\n$$\n\nand the common ratio is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}r} = \\dfrac {a_2}{\\color{black}a_1} = \\dfrac {3 \\left (\\dfrac 1 4 \\right)^2}{3\\left(\\dfrac 1 4 \\right)^1}= {\\color{black}\\dfrac 1 4}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)3(\\frac{1}{4})^{n} &= \\frac{a_{1}}{1 - r} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{3}{4}))/((1 - \\frac{1}{4})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{3}{4}))/((\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186093368 1064 "Suppose we want to evaluate the infinite geometric series \n \n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1} ^ \\infty  3 \\left (\\dfrac 1 4\\right) ^ n.\n$$\n\nThe sum $S_\\infty$ of an infinite geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS_{\\infty} = \\dfrac {\\color{black}a_1} {1 - {\\color{black}r}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{black}r}$ is the common ratio and $\\color{black}a_1$ is the first term. So, we need to determine $\\color{black}a_1$ and $\\color{black}r$ and then plug those values into this formula.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}a_1} = 3 \\left( \\dfrac 1 4 \\right)^1 = {\\color{black}\\dfrac 3 4},\n$$\n\nand the common ratio is \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}r} = \\dfrac {a_2}{\\color{black}a_1} = \\dfrac {3 \\left (\\dfrac 1 4 \\right)^2}{3\\left( \\dfrac 1 4 \\right)^1}= {\\color{black}\\dfrac 1 4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186093452 1064 "We've been using the following formula for the area of the region bounded by a polar curve ![](images/70/7031a0f3ab71df36bc36e925fcf4f04dbfc4feec5f374092855ef8d97b104452.svg) and the rays ![](images/38/38b971de9610efe2ed0de9f10956c73cf5cd967a86a5bcbb076791f369c22bef.svg) and ![](images/81/818e2605a585628c4becd20664b58007dbd2348ca664c80be6a838868a64693a.svg)\n\n![](images/55/55ffcf934c3a9e33a3376fa1796e6fb68ad1198d719db5ea094fe8eb46c5f47d.svg)\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo see where the formula comes from, we can think about breaking up the area bounded by a polar curve into infinitesimally small sectors of circles.\n\nAs shown in the graph below, each sector has a radius of ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) and spans an infinitesimally small angle, ![](images/09/0904a69fa5e6a3a10eaf74b3fdd865db92761eeeffffae1c0badd33ffdf5b34b.svg)\n\n![](images/82/82be86e7092a97ca5b2f1423f32b15030af0c8c63bf47ece8be40b45720ac905.png)\n\nThe ratio of the area of a sector to the full area of the circle must be the same as the ratio of the angle of the sector to a full revolution of the circle. So, if the area of a sector is ![](images/0b/0b2376d17b173024d6f6c82faaff585293e9830ba7ce0764ecf8338adcd14994.svg) then we can set up and solve the following proportion:\n\n![](images/5d/5d318385ab16ee14d784b33c00180a44ade7cee2e97ffeafd290171d0d44868f.svg)\n\nFinally, to find the area under the curve, we can integrate the areas of all the sectors:\n\n![](images/a7/a75940f74a02ceadff519af028783a0e704fba8a73c4abf36c6a371deb56dee7.svg)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186093452 1064 "We've been using the following formula for the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta=\\theta_1$ and $\\theta=\\theta_2{:}$\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2 \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo see where the formula comes from, we can think about breaking up the area bounded by a polar curve into infinitesimally small sectors of circles.\n\nAs shown in the graph below, each sector has a radius of $r$ and spans an infinitesimally small angle, $\\textrm d \\theta.$\n\n![](images/42/42cac6a68ea519fb52c82fffa79d64f02e5cf7bbc2450ae964618ee2dd25827e.png)\n\nThe ratio of the area of a sector to the full area of the circle must be the same as the ratio of the angle of the sector to a full revolution of the circle. So, if the area of a sector is $\\textrm dA,$ then we can set up and solve the following proportion:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dA}{\\pi r^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d\\theta}{2\\pi} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\textrm dA = \\dfrac{1}{2} r^2 \\textrm d\\theta\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the area under the curve, we can integrate the areas of all the sectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186098571 1078 #edb/ref 17592186098573 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186098807 1078 #edb/ref 17592186098808 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100668 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x)+3\n$$\n\nis obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $3$ units *up*.\n\nHence, if the point $(3,4)$ lies on $y=f(x)$, then the point\n\n$$\n(3,4+3) = (3,7)\n$$\n\nlies on\n\n$$\ny=f(x) + 3\n$$\n\n![](images/57/577911187229d6a56a8885e4cea2e25752615ed7bb807768abf4b54c5073d177.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(3,4)$ lies on $y = f(x)$, we have that\n\n$$\nf(3) = 4\n$$\n\nNow, if $x = 3$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= f(3) + 3 \\\\\n&= 4 + 3 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(3,7)$ must lie on\n\n$$\ny = f(x) + 3\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100668 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)+3$ is obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $3$ units *up*. \n\nHence, if the point $(3,4)$ lies on $y=f(x)$, then the point \n\n$$\n(3,4+3) = (3,7)\n$$\n\nlies on $y=f(x) + 3.$\n\n![](images/57/577911187229d6a56a8885e4cea2e25752615ed7bb807768abf4b54c5073d177.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(3,4)$ lies on $y = f(x)$, we have that $f(3) = 4.$\n\nNow, if $x = 3$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(3)+3 \\\\  & =4+3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(3,7)$ must lie on $y = f(x) + 3.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100669 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below and the point $(0,9)$ lies on the curve. Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)-2$.\n\n![](images/b0/b0c3acc57c74ced95e7387e285d9de1c629e7724fe9e111b13df209198069e8d.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100669 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below and the point $(0,9)$ lies on the curve. Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)-2.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0c3acc57c74ced95e7387e285d9de1c629e7724fe9e111b13df209198069e8d.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100669 1074 "The graph of $y = f(x)$ passes through the point $(0, 9)$ as shown in the diagram.\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)-2$ can be obtained from $y=f(x)$ by translating it by $2$ units *down*. So, it must pass through the point $(0,7)$.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27de6227dee35cb8a7eaf2873409dfd6ad8c3c5f8d0cbd0de9ac223925b3fd76.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100669 1074 "The graph of $y = f(x)$ passes through the point $(0, 9)$ as shown in the diagram. \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)-2$ can be obtained from $y=f(x)$ by translating it by $2$ units *down*. So, it must pass through the point $(0,7).$\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27de6227dee35cb8a7eaf2873409dfd6ad8c3c5f8d0cbd0de9ac223925b3fd76.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100670 1071 "The point $A$ with coordinates $(2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x)$ and the point $B$ with coordinates $(2,-4)$ lies on the curve $y = g(x)$. If the curve $y = g(x)$ is related to $y = f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x) = f(x) + k$, then what is the value of $k$?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100670 1071 "The point $A$ with coordinates $(2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x)$ and the point $B$ with coordinates $(2,-4)$ lies on the curve $y = g(x).$ If the curve $y = g(x)$ is related to $y = f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x) = f(x) + k$, then what is the value of $k?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100670 1074 "First, notice that the point $B(2, -4)$ lies $3$ units *below* the point $A(2, -1)$. So, the graph of $y = g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by shifting it $3$ units *down*.\n\n![](images/46/4656ae5ff75132fc9b760b6a8f73b0b434a8f66ecd621fa974a265df1d0d8a2c.png)\n\nAs a result, $k = -3$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100670 1074 "First, notice that the point $B(2, -4)$ lies $3$ units *below* the point $A(2, -1).$ So, the graph of $y = g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by shifting it $3$ units *down*. \n\n![](images/46/4656ae5ff75132fc9b760b6a8f73b0b434a8f66ecd621fa974a265df1d0d8a2c.png)\n\nAs a result, $k = -3.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100708 1071 "![](images/30/303b6a70330995c124cdfea0bc125febd5ebcf80cb79c8656086d57f3a6f272f.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{2}f(x)$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100708 1071 "![](images/30/303b6a70330995c124cdfea0bc125febd5ebcf80cb79c8656086d57f3a6f272f.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{2}f(x).$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100708 1074 "To graph the function\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{2}f(x)\n$$\n\nwe take the graph of $y = f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y = f(x)$ by $2$.\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71b6584d644419a56b2cebb64124510e9f15cca8a4f19e6c1a8d4b6f1441cd0a.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100708 1074 "To graph the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{2}f(x),$ we take the graph of $y = f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y = f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71b6584d644419a56b2cebb64124510e9f15cca8a4f19e6c1a8d4b6f1441cd0a.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100709 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y=4f(x)$.\n\n![](images/49/495e5e02f89dfa125cb95095088cb80cf3e83ab9e6dae619494e900cbe1dc346.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100709 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y=4f(x).$\n\n![](images/49/495e5e02f89dfa125cb95095088cb80cf3e83ab9e6dae619494e900cbe1dc346.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100709 1074 "To graph the function $y = 4f(x)$, we take the graph of $y = f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y = f(x)$ by $4$.\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b20c1355ae0b2e8afc7e7ce701662e0aaf2a6fdcd8e43342ae057e8bd9250e9.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100709 1074 "To graph the function $y = 4f(x),$ we take the graph of $y = f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y = f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b20c1355ae0b2e8afc7e7ce701662e0aaf2a6fdcd8e43342ae057e8bd9250e9.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100711 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-2, 2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$. Find a point that must lie on the graph of $y= 7f(x)$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100711 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-2, 2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Find a point that must lie on the graph of $y= 7f(x).$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100711 1074 "The function $y = 7f(x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y = f(x)$ by a factor of $7$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $7$.\n\nTherefore, the point $(-2,2)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, 7 \\cdot 2\\right) = (-2, 14)\n$$\n\nand this point must lie on the graph of $y = 7f(x)$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100711 1074 "The function $y = 7f(x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y = f(x)$ by a factor of $7$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-2,2)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $\\left(-2, 7 \\cdot 2\\right) = (-2 , 14),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y = 7f(x).$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100712 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x) +2$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$?\n\n![](images/f5/f5404d5fbf52098eaa7161f0219e4df98a3ae9f5b6b7b6b0220089d62abff82d.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100712 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x) +2$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)?$\n\n![](images/f5/f5404d5fbf52098eaa7161f0219e4df98a3ae9f5b6b7b6b0220089d62abff82d.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100712 1074 "The graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x) + 2\n$$\n\ncan be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by translating $2$ units *up*.\n\nSo, the graph $y = f(x)$ would be $2$ units *below* the graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x) + 2\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0ce67d91fd6c15d97e882c785392f001a4f38ed9bedd50d8631b34e4d5543adc.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186100712 1074 "The graph of $y = f(x) + 2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by translating $2$ units *up*. \n\nSo, the graph $y = f(x)$ would be $2$ units *below* the graph of $y = f(x) + 2.$\n\n![](images/0c/0ce67d91fd6c15d97e882c785392f001a4f38ed9bedd50d8631b34e4d5543adc.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529163 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529184 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529146 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529106 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529108 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529167 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529192 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100985 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529101 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100985 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529103 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100986 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529131 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100986 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529183 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100987 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529105 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186100987 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529186 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186101091 1050 420.0 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186101092 1050 420.0 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186101206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186101206 1071 "![](images/82/82012efd1fbf78238ce1b6ecc49819283976db8f39ac5f57ec232a5eda8029dc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above and the point $(0, 1)$ lies on the curve. Which of the following is the graph of $y = f(x) + 2$?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186101206 1071 "![](images/82/82012efd1fbf78238ce1b6ecc49819283976db8f39ac5f57ec232a5eda8029dc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above and the point $(0,1)$ lies on the curve. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)+2?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186101206 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(0,1)$ as shown in the diagram. \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)+2$ can be obtained from $y=f(x)$ by translating it by $2$ units *up*. So, it must pass through the point $(0,3).$\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f0a46e8f6ef1daac4acbaf49e5fb1ffc21046cbfc6260fe6dbe51d1814a1c12.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186101246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186101246 1071 "![](images/9a/9a6425ad44f0330287193b8495260033697d862c2a7ce336ac44aeb5bab783fc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y = 2f(x)$?\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186101246 1071 "![](images/9a/9a6425ad44f0330287193b8495260033697d862c2a7ce336ac44aeb5bab783fc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=2f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186101246 1074 "To graph the function $y=2f(x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/a1/a1aa0219a3afeb77d4b04aab0232c1fb75541564fca2291a46d3700831236d16.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186101945 1064 "Suppose that we have the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. What would the graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+2\n$$\n\nlook like, and how does it relate to the original curve?\n\n![](images/1a/1a55ebd7e9dc656f3a2488debf14e63e655ed7548b76f63c26498f09d96d65ca.png)\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+2\n$$\n\nwith the same $x$-value and a $y$-value that is two units greater. So let's create a table of points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=f(x)+2[/math] \\\\ (-2, 0) & (-2, 2) \\\\ (0, 1.5) & (0, 3.5) \\\\ (1, 2) & (1, 4) \\\\ (3,-3) & (3,-1)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery point on the graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+2\n$$\n\nis precisely two units above a point on the graph of $y=f(x)$. The graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+2\n$$\n\nis an **upward vertical translation** of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by two units.\n\n![](images/66/6636d65acf9cd34d818d3d9e12a7b7de36dec4ceb70c7503a08751f971868855.png)\n\nOn the other hand, for each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$ with the same $x$-value and a $y$-value that is two units smaller. So let's create a table of points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=f(x)-2[/math] \\\\ (-2, 0) & (-2,-2) \\\\ (0, 1.5) & (0,-0.5) \\\\ (1, 2) & (1, 0) \\\\ (3,-3) & (3,-5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery point on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$ is two units below a point on the graph of $y=f(x)$. The graph of $y=f(x)-2$ is a **downward vertical translation** of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by two units.\n\n![](images/72/72588aaa92f5b4bf9923c381cf5ad28ebb7288e77f6442549d0ffe1fdac3dc48.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186101945 1064 "Suppose that we have the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. What would the graph of $y=f(x)+2$ look like, and how does it relate to the original curve?\n\n![](images/1a/1a55ebd7e9dc656f3a2488debf14e63e655ed7548b76f63c26498f09d96d65ca.png)\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$ with the same $x$-value and a $y$-value that is two units greater. So let's create a table of points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)+2[/math]} \\\\ (-2,0) & (-2,2) \\\\ (0,1.5) & (0,3.5) \\\\ (1,2) & (1,4) \\\\ (3,-3) & (3,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery point on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$ is precisely two units above a point on the graph of $y=f(x)$. The graph of $y=f(x)+2$ is an **upward vertical translation** of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by two units.\n\n![](images/66/6636d65acf9cd34d818d3d9e12a7b7de36dec4ceb70c7503a08751f971868855.png)\n\nOn the other hand, for each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$ with the same $x$-value and a $y$-value that is two units smaller. So let's create a table of points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)-2[/math]} \\\\ (-2,0) & (-2,-2) \\\\ (0,1.5) & (0,-0.5) \\\\ (1,2) & (1,0) \\\\ (3,-3) & (3,-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery point on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$ is two units below a point on the graph of $y=f(x)$. The graph of $y=f(x)-2$ is a **downward vertical translation** of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by two units.\n\n![](images/72/72588aaa92f5b4bf9923c381cf5ad28ebb7288e77f6442549d0ffe1fdac3dc48.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186101970 1064 "When we multiply a function by a constant, the graph either stretches out or compresses. Transforming a function in this way is called **scaling**.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below.\n\n![](images/21/21f9b06e470ca0e94605eb72c5a12d2a1f2ec56645588a960f12be81ad8252ec.png)\n\nThe following table lists four sets of points. The set of points in the left-hand column correspond to the graph of $y=f(x)$, and the set on the right-hand side corresponds to $y=2f(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=2f(x)[/math] \\\\ (1, 2) & (1, 4) \\\\ (4,-3) & (4,-6) \\\\ (0, 2) & (0, 4) \\\\ (-2, 0) & (-2, 0)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$, there is a point on the graph of $y=2f(x)$ with the same $x$-value and *double* the $y$-value.\n\nNow that we have four points on the graph of $y=2f(x)$, let's plot the full graph.\n\n![](images/26/262b6515b995f6fdcf7b959dc62d9599b71476471fbc2bde394dcebfd47cabc0.png)\n\nWe say that the graph of $y=2f(x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ **vertically stretched** by a **scale factor** of $2$.\n\nWe can also say that the graph of $y=2f(x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ stretched by a factor of $2$ **parallel to the $\\mathbf y$-axis.**" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186101970 1064 "When we multiply a function by a constant, the graph either stretches out or compresses. Transforming a function in this way is called **scaling**.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below.\n\n![](images/21/21f9b06e470ca0e94605eb72c5a12d2a1f2ec56645588a960f12be81ad8252ec.png)\n\nThe following table lists four sets of points. The set of points in the left-hand column correspond to the graph of $y=f(x),$ and the set on the right-hand side corresponds to $y=2f(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=2f(x)[/math]} \\\\ (1,2) & (1,4) \\\\ (4,-3) & (4,-6) \\\\ (0,2) & (0,4) &  \\\\ (-2,0) & (-2,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x),$ there is a point on the graph of $y=2f(x)$ with the same $x$-value and *double* the $y$-value. \n\nNow that we have four points on the graph of $y=2f(x),$ let's plot the full graph.\n\n![](images/26/262b6515b995f6fdcf7b959dc62d9599b71476471fbc2bde394dcebfd47cabc0.png)\n\nWe say that the graph of $y=2f(x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ **vertically stretched** by a **scale factor** of $2.$ \n\nWe can also say that the graph of $y=2f(x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ stretched by a factor of $2$ **parallel to the $\\mathbf y$-axis.**" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186101972 1064 "Consider the solid curve $y=f(x)$ and the dashed curve\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+b\n$$\n\nshown below. What is the value of $b$?\n\n![](images/b0/b058da1ea31e45f3029f1542642d802a35da7280f315a37ea92044e5c322fbd4.png)\n\nThe graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+b\n$$\n\ncan be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by translating it $6$ units down. So, $b=-6$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186101972 1064 "Consider the solid curve $y=f(x)$ and the dashed curve $y=f(x)+b$ shown below. What is the value of $b?$\n\n![](images/b0/b058da1ea31e45f3029f1542642d802a35da7280f315a37ea92044e5c322fbd4.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)+b$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by translating it $6$ units down. So, $b=-6.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186112039 1075 #edb/ref 17592186112040 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186112039 1075 #edb/ref 17592186112041 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186112039 1075 #edb/ref 17592186112042 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186112039 1075 #edb/ref 17592186112043 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186112039 1075 #edb/ref 17592186112044 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186112039 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528406 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186112039 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528449 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186112039 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528698 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186112039 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528835 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186112039 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528894 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186112039 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528894 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115100 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac {x^2 - 9} {(x + 3)(x+2)}$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186115100 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac {x^2 - 9} {(x + 3)(x+2)}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115100 1074 "The numerator is a difference of squares. If we factor it and then cancel out any common factors that appear in both the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {x^2 - 9} {(x + 3)(x+2)}  & = \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {(x + 3)(x - 3)} {(x + 3)(x+2)}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {\\cancel{(x + 3)}(x - 3)} {\\cancel{(x + 3)}(x+2)}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {x - 3} {x+2} &. \\\t\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115100 1074 "The numerator is a difference of squares. If we factor it and then cancel out any common factors that appear in both the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {x^2 - 9} {(x + 3)(x+2)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {(x + 3)(x - 3)} {(x + 3)(x+2)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {\\cancel{(x + 3)}(x - 3)} {\\cancel{(x + 3)}(x+2)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x - 3} {x+2} &. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186115101 1074 "The numerator can be factored as\n\n$$\nx^2+x-6= (x+3)(x-2)\n$$\n\nCancelling out any common factors that appear in the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} + x - 6}{x - 2} &=  \\\\\n((x + 3)(x - 2))/(x - 2) &=  \\\\\n((x + 3)(x - 2))/((x - 2)) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{x + 3}{1} &=  \\\\\n&= x + 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186115101 1074 "The numerator can be factored as $x^2+x-6= (x+3)(x-2).$ Cancelling out any common factors that appear in the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{x-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)(x-2)}{x-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{x+3}{1} & = \\\\ x+3 & .\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115102 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{4(a+b)} {2a^2+4ab+2b^2}$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186115102 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{4(a+b)} {2a^2+4ab+2b^2}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115102 1074 "Here, we take out a factor of $2$ from the denominator, and then use the polynomial factorization\n\n$$\na^2+2ab+b^2 = (a+b)^2\n$$\n\nCancelling out any common factors that appear in both the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{4(a+b)} {2a^2+4ab+2b^2} &=\\dfrac{4(a+b)} {2\\left(a^2+2ab+b^2\\right)} \\\\ &=\\dfrac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot(a+b)} {2(a+b)(a+b)} \\\\ &=\\dfrac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot\\cancel{(a+b)}} {\\cancel{2}\\cancel{(a+b)}(a+b)} \\\\ &=\\dfrac {2} {a+b}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186115102 1074 "Here, we take out a factor of $2$ from the denominator, and then use the polynomial factorization $a^2+2ab+b^2 = (a+b)^2.$ Cancelling out any common factors that appear in both the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{4(a+b)} {2a^2+4ab+2b^2}\n\t\t&=\\dfrac{4(a+b)} {2\\left(a^2+2ab+b^2\\right)} \\\n                &=\\dfrac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot(a+b)} {2(a+b)(a+b)} \\\n\t\t&=\\dfrac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot\\cancel{(a+b)}} {\\cancel{2}\\cancel{(a+b)}(a+b)}\\\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {2} {a+b}.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115104 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{x^2-4}{x^2-5x+6}$." 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186115104 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{x^2-4}{x^2-5x+6}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115104 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator, canceling out any common factors that appear in both.\n\n![](images/39/39c702fb9697e87abfb8bbfb1a4bfbec813a0f4ffbe47d3ea90b56adfdd371a0.svg)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115104 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator, canceling out any common factors that appear in both.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} - 4}{x^{2} - 5x + 6} &= ((x - 2)(x + 2))/((x - 3)(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x + 2))/((x - 3)(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x + 2}{x - 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 false] [17592186115271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529172 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529173 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115272 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529098 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115272 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529154 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115273 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529110 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115273 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529169 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186115279 1038 #edb/ref 1759218