6529084 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528344 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529084 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528867 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529084 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529084 1077 "field-3" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529084 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528867 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529084 1079 #uuid "f99f19ef-a061-5ea6-8ad8-153820c7f959" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529085 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528341 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529085 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528542 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529085 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529085 1077 "field-2" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529085 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528542 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529085 1079 #uuid "f99f332a-f486-5c22-ada6-441f0984caf5" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528471 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528538 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528588 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529086 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528720 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529086 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529086 1077 "field-7" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529086 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528588 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529086 1079 #uuid "ff4301ae-bf90-5f16-a0cd-99a316c4b8d2" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529057 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529087 1069 "q-141165" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529087 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529087 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529087 1072 #uuid "01f92ee9-73cd-5c08-adfa-addc2317087e" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529087 1074 "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)+5\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (-2))+2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+5+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528968 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529088 1069 "q-50239" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529088 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529088 1071 "![](images/96/96f465c4da3f2e482fa0224e23564d27e363a727602ec05ed6daad8213807211.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=5$ and $x=\\sqrt{3},$ where $x\\ge 0,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529088 1072 #uuid "0b6da66a-d01e-54fd-b6f1-7c1c4b4f3048" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529088 1074 "Writing our first function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=5\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{5-2{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{5-2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3 & =5-2{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$ \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/54/54fbb219933d282480f70d8f87cbb8225c126979c58a5be6faed0a98eaf97c51.png)\n\n \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=\\sqrt{5-2{y}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[\\sqrt{5-2{y}^{2}}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}([5-2{y}^{2}]-3)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528954 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529089 1069 "q-123369" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529089 1071 "![](images/fb/fb3afbc1bebf1abc77b0495135e02b26da362ffb346aa261d42ba43dca94ec66.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $x={e}^{{y}^{2}}$ and $x={e}^{y+2},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the $y$-axis?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529089 1072 #uuid "0bef34f9-b7a4-5afd-919c-8a8bc54bd07d" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529089 1074 "Let $f(y)={e}^{{y}^{2}}$ and $g(y)={e}^{y+2}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {e}^{{y}^{2}} & ={e}^{y+2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =y+2 \\\\ {y}^{2}-y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/b6/b6db4894c0cf0204ae676a0350771cf47359031f30fb975d48d5864b1592e673.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)={e}^{{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)={e}^{y+2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[{e}^{y+2}]}^{2}-{[{e}^{{y}^{2}}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({e}^{2y+4}-{e}^{2{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529081 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529090 1069 "q-123488" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529090 1071 "![](images/b2/b2888d7fef98dacad56792a627ef1554ed28def615f1431de35d3ba9820fe0de.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $x={y}^{2}+2$ and $y=2-x,$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529090 1072 #uuid "10f20a34-5dca-5f03-b661-36330ae0c9f9" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529090 1074 "Writing our second function in terms of $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=2-x\\,⟹\\,x=2-y.\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)={y}^{2}+2$ and $g(y)=2-y.$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{2}+2 & =2-y \\\\ {y}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ y(y+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=0.$    \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/af/afe7bb6fbc5f523e04bdd8c5bc9e899af356729da0e4c7822bd7619dfeb3cb95.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)={y}^{2}+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)=2-y.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[2-y]}^{2}-{[{y}^{2}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-4y+{y}^{2}-({y}^{4}+4{y}^{2}+4))\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{y}^{4}-3{y}^{2}-4y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2})|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\pi [0-(\\frac{1}{5}+1-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528973 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529091 1069 "q-106044" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529091 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529091 1071 "![](images/cf/cf3840cca56d90bc6b3981d15ead9c4ae45665693ef5c7794e65574204e632db.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y={x}^{2}-3x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529091 1072 #uuid "1100bf2f-961a-5344-8257-63842e33b322" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529091 1074 "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}-3x+k \\\\ y & =(0)^{2}-3(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=2$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2).$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529043 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529092 1069 "q-183269" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529092 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529092 1071 "$\\,$ $x$ is not both a multiple of $5$ and a multiple of $9.$\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is a multiple of $5$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x$ is a multiple of $9$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529092 1072 #uuid "12705253-40c6-5f3d-bde2-d9b9b3c2a300" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529092 1074 "The given sentence is the negation of the following sentence: \n\n$\\,$ $x$ is both a multiple of $5$ and a multiple of $9.$\n\nThe sentence above can be expressed symbolically as $P∧Q,$ and we can negate this sentence using the symbol \"$¬$\".\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is expressed symbolically as\n\n$$\n¬(P∧Q).\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528977 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529093 1069 "q-50498" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529093 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529093 1071 "Given that ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-\\frac{1}{10}$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529093 1072 #uuid "14f76056-5e3c-539d-8f82-855878eb07d3" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529093 1074 "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{5}{2}}{(1-(-\\frac{1}{10}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{5}{2}}{(\\frac{11}{10})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{10}{11} \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529003 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529029 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529072 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529094 1069 "q-183300" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529094 1071 "$\\,$ $a<8$ and $a>2.$\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529094 1072 #uuid "16975edf-5610-5167-bf92-0dd71021b831" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529094 1074 "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$a<8$ and $a>2$ \" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(a<8\\text{ and }a>2) & ≡¬(a<8∧a>2) \\\\  & ≡¬(a<8)∨¬(a>2) \\\\  & ≡a\\ge 8∨a\\le 2 \\\\  & ≡a\\ge 8\\text{ or }a\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529026 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529095 1069 "q-122546" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529095 1071 "![](images/8c/8ce8a8061ec087c39ce93d73d0566832f33c4e6ce5c2f5185170c9372a0d88fa.png)\n\nConsider the finite region above bounded by $y=2x+2$ and $y=\\sqrt{4x+4},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x\\text{-axis.}$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529095 1072 #uuid "17abd4d5-764f-50c1-82c3-4fcec4b8a574" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529095 1074 "Let $f(x)=2x+2$ and $g(x)=\\sqrt{4x+4}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2x+2 & =\\sqrt{4x+4} \\\\ 2(x+1) & =\\sqrt{4x+4} \\\\ 4(x+1)^{2} & =4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1 & =x+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/0a/0a6bdcc071500ac8dd40143c3219a14c80fc73d543d7307cb2c880a22db793a3.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=f(x)=2x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=g(x)=\\sqrt{4x+4}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[\\sqrt{4x+4}]}^{2}-{[2x+2]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[4x+4-(2(x+1))^{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[4(x+1)-4({x}^{2}+2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[x+1-({x}^{2}+2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\pi (0-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528941 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529096 1069 "q-355834" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529096 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.98 & -0.67 \\\\ 0.52 & 1.49 \\\\ 1.14 & -0.36 \\\\ -0.69 & -0.46\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.56 & -0.17 & 0.71 & -0.393 \\\\ 0.682 & -0.53 & 0.486 & 0.135 \\\\ 0.264 & 0.821 & 0.485 & 0.144 \\\\ -0.39 & -0.126 & 0.159 & 0.898\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.085 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.292 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & 0.708 \\\\ 0.708 & -0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.98 & -0.67\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.98 & -0.67 \\\\ 0.52 & 1.49 \\\\ 1.14 & -0.36 \\\\ -0.69 & -0.46\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529096 1072 #uuid "17f34adc-72ed-5271-a021-3a7f1d773e6b" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529096 1074 "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option II represents the projection of only the first datapoint (the first row of $X$) onto the first principal component. Therefore, it produces a single scalar value rather than the full one-dimensional dataset.\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of the full dataset $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529061 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529097 1069 "q-24639" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529097 1071 "![](images/28/28032a665a1266f06292394bc9a238b196c25ef9e8d275009e3d7587a1511c6e.png)\n\nFind the roots of the parabola shown above." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529097 1072 #uuid "1834012f-4a0b-5e03-8f32-bee5b9948e5b" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529097 1074 "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, the root is $x=-1.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529064 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529098 1069 "q-32322" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529098 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{4{x}^{2}-8x+4}{4x-4}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529098 1072 #uuid "1950cd5e-6ddb-5e2d-959e-224740d86f48" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529098 1074 "The numerator can be factored as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-8x+4=4({x}^{2}-2x+1)=4(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nCanceling out any common factors that appear in the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}-8x+4}{4x-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(x-1)^{2}}{4(x-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(x-1)(x-1)}{4(x-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4(x-1)}(x-1)}{\\cancel{4(x-1)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{1} & = \\\\ x-1. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528962 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529011 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529084 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529099 1069 "q-183297" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529099 1071 "$\\,$ $x\\le 0\\text{ and }y$ is odd.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529099 1072 #uuid "1e781df2-007a-5016-ba10-713cbfb69f20" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529099 1074 "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x\\le 0\\text{ and }y$ is odd\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\le 0\\text{ and }y\\text{ is odd}) & ≡¬(x\\le 0∧y\\text{ is odd}) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\le 0)∨¬(y\\text{ is odd}) \\\\  & ≡x>0∨y\\text{ is even} \\\\  & ≡x>0\\text{ or }y\\text{ is even}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528961 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529100 1069 "q-105994" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529100 1071 "What are the coordinates of the minimum point of the function $y=(x-2)^{2}?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529100 1072 #uuid "1f5ba24c-6238-510f-bebb-ee4c2a2d372a" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529100 1074 "In general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^{2}$ looks like the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^{2}$ is located at $(h,0).$\n\nFor the given function $y=(x-2)^{2},$ we have $h=2.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ after being translated $2$ units *right*. \n\n![](images/ae/aed4cce9a698952f9d24e386249c88023d4e56a353f2d7e0c6b75f3bec0cdf4d.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(2,0),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528965 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529101 1069 "q-110278" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529101 1071 "![](images/5f/5f93b8d3dd3c88d70d6fe0e5a73bd6da1aeb4eae4811d7aaeb47d5ea620279a5.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=4f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529101 1072 #uuid "227b11b3-a4e0-591c-bdef-0816b0978926" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529101 1074 "To graph the function $y=4f(x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4b0c34ba1ed9036d8f42693e1c087f10df75e7fe27b1ad23d8885467de1cfab.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528997 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529102 1069 "q-124357" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529102 1071 "Which of the following gives the total area enclosed by graph of the polar curve $r=9\\theta +5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529102 1072 #uuid "2437a359-bd35-5179-92d6-ee80bc33d39e" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529102 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=9\\theta +5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(9\\theta +5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528949 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529103 1069 "q-111029" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529103 1071 "![](images/b8/b8150a85386a2ebea9119820d25f227a6cb33aef8dd09b519feef4e8f70a4013.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=2f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529103 1072 #uuid "257d80f9-98d7-5f29-b46f-5e7f007c2b82" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529103 1074 "To graph the function $y=2f(x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a88c2321811017dcedf2c83178b47dcf8f314926e1fb2ef4273681def639f2a0.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529016 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529104 1069 "q-355831" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529104 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.27 & 0.39 \\\\ -0.99 & -1.23 \\\\ -0.13 & -0.28 \\\\ 1.39 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.035 & 0.86 & 0.132 & 0.491 \\\\ -0.657 & -0.314 & -0.208 & 0.653 \\\\ -0.121 & -0.196 & 0.969 & 0.092 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.351 & -0.032 & 0.569\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.389 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.542 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.035 \\\\ -0.657 \\\\ -0.121 \\\\ 0.743\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.389$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.035 \\\\ 0.86 \\\\ 0.132 \\\\ 0.491\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.389$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529104 1072 #uuid "2633d33a-0706-5b9b-a985-6a0050cda4a1" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529104 1074 "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Option II represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option III uses the values of the first row of $U$ multiplied by ${\\sigma }_{1}.$ However, the projection corresponds to the first column of $U.$ Thus, using the row vector is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529050 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529105 1069 "q-111185" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529105 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(3,6)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529105 1072 #uuid "2708eacc-b12f-5049-bb93-6886b57484fd" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529105 1074 "The function $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)$ represents a compression of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,6)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $(3,\\frac{6}{3})=(3,2),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529019 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529106 1069 "q-108226" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529106 1071 "![](images/71/7187ce3dfce771f3897b7073704b49fa8926bb8c1a16cd18af68e02978d2a526.png)\n\nConsider the solid curve $y=f(x)$ and the dashed curve $y=f(x)+c$ shown above. What is the value of $c?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529106 1072 #uuid "2b584399-1501-573d-8e9c-16d0bbc631a2" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529106 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x)+c$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by translating it $7$ units *up*. So, $c=7.$\n\n![](images/a5/a5322569c974effb49773ee06a38da3f1953e0d9fc55464d8fae367e45dc15af.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528947 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529107 1069 "q-352331" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529107 1071 "![](images/e3/e3f2350ba3651e928c65e1a78908a8f8e93cb690585a3fa16b723d1cf38a5024.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529107 1072 #uuid "2ba92fa4-3450-58d7-9f90-59493b716e4d" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529107 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{9}{2}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9}{2}\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}{8}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+4) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{9\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529007 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529108 1069 "q-70595" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529108 1071 "![](images/62/62474d31ab2981d6173671acb875ec401a51c230994e60e10421d385f2e099a9.png)\n\nConsider the solid curve $y=f(x)$ and the dashed curve $y=f(x)+b$ shown above. What is the value of $b?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529108 1072 #uuid "3149eba2-f75e-505c-909c-9beed59b368d" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529108 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x)+b$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by translating it $6$ units *down*. So, $b=-6.$\n\n![](images/3f/3fcbd6ce5ea6543fea0038df30e8f31db18c82ceefa975aaaec1854d85766eab.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528946 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529027 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529109 1069 "q-355844" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529109 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.74 & 0.61 & 0.89 \\\\ 0.38 & 0 & 0.61 \\\\ -0.92 & -0.03 & -0.87 \\\\ -1.2 & -1.61 & -1.28 \\\\ 1.01 & 1.02 & 0.65\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.389 & 0.167 & -0.312 & 0.84 & -0.133 \\\\ -0.174 & 0.453 & -0.384 & -0.192 & 0.762 \\\\ 0.321 & -0.749 & -0.348 & 0.232 & 0.402 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.384 & 0.359 & 0.419 & 0.22 \\\\ -0.464 & -0.242 & 0.712 & 0.166 & 0.438\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.329 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.311 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.591 & 0.31 & 0.745 \\\\ -0.558 & -0.823 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.582 & 0.475 & -0.66\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $95%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529109 1072 #uuid "3158298f-c612-536d-8b25-00f57fa2f77e" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529109 1074 "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.329,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=0.9,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.311.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.329}^{2}}{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}+{0.311}^{2}}≈92.4%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}}{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}+{0.311}^{2}}≈99.2%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $92.4%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $99.2%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/e2/e2af29d0509f7ae3e3826cd35d3190b478e9c2415c00fc0e3798800d33381c59.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $95%$ of the variance." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528964 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529110 1069 "q-29819" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529110 1071 "$\\frac{a+b}{7{a}^{2}-7{b}^{2}}=$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529110 1072 #uuid "33d475f4-4da4-572d-a596-bce698fbb9fc" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529110 1074 "The denominator can be factored as the difference of squares: \n\n$$\n7{a}^{2}-7{b}^{2}=7({a}^{2}-{b}^{2})=7(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nCanceling out any common factors that appear in both the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a+b}{7{a}^{2}-7{b}^{2}} & =\\frac{a+b}{7(a+b)(a-b)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(a+b)}}{7\\cancel{(a+b)}(a-b)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7(a-b)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529028 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529111 1069 "q-355838" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529111 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.33 & 1.18 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.88 \\\\ 1.49 & 0.48 \\\\ -0.66 & -0.77\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 & 0.813 & 0.505 & 0.021 \\\\ -0.469 & -0.212 & 0.634 & -0.578 \\\\ 0.676 & -0.54 & 0.473 & 0.168 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.061 & 0.345 & 0.798\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.065 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.316 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.709 & -0.705 \\\\ 0.705 & 0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ 0.705\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.33 & 1.18 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.88 \\\\ 1.49 & 0.48 \\\\ -0.66 & -0.77\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ 0.705\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ -0.469 \\\\ 0.676 \\\\ -0.49\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.065$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529111 1072 #uuid "3689d124-7069-5ac1-b495-7dcfa807134a" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529111 1074 "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option II corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529015 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529112 1069 "q-123912" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529112 1071 "![](images/98/98bc6f2ea4d71368226f0f784d629e98949e07005bc08158206de35a87bea520.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,$ as shown above." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529112 1072 #uuid "3c3c2375-fac6-5465-bbbf-42c39eaebb70" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529112 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=2\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(9-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi })) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{4}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{8}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-9(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{9}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi }{8}-9+\\frac{9}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi -72}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528987 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529113 1069 "q-9640" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529113 1071 "![](images/7c/7ca646111a7708da96fa1bea8aca056abf0c45f9a4295e2beb27181d704aa814.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{8},$ as shown above." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529113 1072 #uuid "3c7bf914-2de6-57ee-85dd-b40dcc8d6cfc" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529113 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}-\\frac{\\pi }{8})+\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529041 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529114 1069 "q-122558" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529114 1071 "![](images/b9/b9f1ea82756eed4a815afb54defd8425631a57a0bcd1dfaba2dbf5b24b43b518.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $x=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ and $x=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the $y$-axis?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529114 1072 #uuid "3d1bfa1c-c252-5832-a4ec-b38acfcd7492" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529114 1074 "Let $f(y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ and $g(y)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ (y-3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=1$ and $y=3.$ \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/54/54278318429e01bc3fa2c0325fea7c7a918aacf096b1510d8acc87d3ffc53a73.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}({[3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}-{[y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2}-{y}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(9-{y}^{2})(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529082 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529115 1069 "q-44092" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529115 1071 "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529115 1072 #uuid "3ec696af-4927-5944-9a02-9b2829e4a66e" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529115 1074 "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (3\\cdot (-4))+(-1)\\cdot k \\\\  & =-4-48-k \\\\  & =-52-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ -52-k & =0 \\\\ k & =-52\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528999 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529014 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529116 1069 "q-355908" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529116 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.36 & 1.7 & 0.25 & 1.05 \\\\ 0.8 & -0.33 & -1.37 & -0.53 \\\\ -0.51 & -0.23 & 0.05 & -0.61 \\\\ -1.18 & -0.96 & -0.32 & -1.01 \\\\ 1.25 & -0.18 & 1.4 & 1.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.477 & 0.704 & -0.239 & 0.135 & 0.448 \\\\ -0.291 & -0.357 & -0.764 & 0.049 & 0.449 \\\\ -0.217 & 0.106 & 0.24 & -0.83 & 0.442 \\\\ -0.55 & 0.135 & 0.445 & 0.529 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.582 & -0.589 & 0.323 & 0.104 & 0.447\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.041 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.967 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.671 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.268 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.356 & -0.757 & -0.46 & -0.298 \\\\ 0.454 & 0.644 & -0.416 & -0.454 \\\\ 0.493 & -0.1 & 0.783 & -0.366 \\\\ 0.652 & 0.041 & -0.052 & 0.755\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529116 1072 #uuid "439142f8-6932-5f54-8d84-ca7931d891df" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529116 1074 "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.041,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.967,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.671,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.268.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈57.9%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈82.1%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈99.6%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $57.9%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $82.1%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $99.6%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/5e/5eb43d1adcfa48f0fd66cd73ea05b40a9e937399457ba8614f380502c1c632dc.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529002 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529117 1069 "q-11107" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529117 1071 "![](images/0f/0f94662dcae88371bb47d9748d002e38c6e521b0ba689e31e782ce288d266a58.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2$ and ${x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=1,$ where $x\\ge 0,$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529117 1072 #uuid "4870784d-cfa2-5882-86d4-3e6f72784ac7" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529117 1074 "Given that $x\\ge 0,$ writing our functions in terms of $y,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{2+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2+{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2+{y}^{2} & =1+2{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$    \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/41/4177abe03f95230a3176738cddbc556d2e4c3703fe72c1391a53c1ecb5f82181.png)\n\n  \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=\\sqrt{2+{y}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[\\sqrt{2+{y}^{2}}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}((2+{y}^{2})-(1+2{y}^{2}))\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3})|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(1-\\frac{1}{3})-((-1)+\\frac{1}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528945 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529118 1069 "q-5362" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529118 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2n}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529118 1072 #uuid "499dc119-15d3-5a33-9631-8bc1b2a65d1c" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529118 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2(1)}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2(2)}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{4}}{\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}}={(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{4}{25}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}}{1-\\frac{4}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{7}{10}(\\frac{4}{25})}{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}\\cdot \\frac{4}{25}\\cdot \\frac{25}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528972 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529119 1069 "q-350625" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529119 1071 "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-7)(0.3)^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529119 1072 #uuid "4db8cfdc-3e22-5283-b109-341d9daf5b09" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529119 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=(-7)(0.3)^{2(1)}=(-7)(0.3)^{2}=(-7)(0.09)=-0.63,\n$$\n\nand the second term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=(-7)(0.3)^{2(2)}=(-7)(0.3)^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-7)(0.3)^{4}}{(-7)(0.3)^{2}}=(0.3)^{2}=0.09.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-7)(0.3)^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-0.63)}{1-0.09} \\\\  & =-\\frac{0.63}{0.91} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529012 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529024 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529085 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529120 1069 "q-183299" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529120 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529120 1071 "$\\,$ $x\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\text{ and }y>\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529120 1072 #uuid "4e83f3e0-1564-59fb-a764-5da5f052add0" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529120 1074 "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\text{ and }y>\\frac{1}{2}$\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\text{ and }y>\\frac{1}{2}) & ≡¬(x\\le \\frac{1}{3}∧y>\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\le \\frac{1}{3})∨¬(y>\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ≡x>\\frac{1}{3}∨y\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ≡x>\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ or }y\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528988 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529030 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529053 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529121 1069 "q-183346" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529121 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529121 1071 "$\\,$ $x=2$ or $y\\ne -2.$\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529121 1072 #uuid "502d8fb9-af55-51a0-accb-d73687bf175c" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529121 1074 "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x=2$ or $y\\ne -2$\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x=2\\text{ or }y\\ne -2) & ≡¬(x=2∨y\\ne -2) \\\\  & ≡¬(x=2)∧¬(y\\ne -2) \\\\  & ≡x\\ne 2∧y=-2 \\\\  & ≡x\\ne 2\\text{ and }y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529038 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529122 1069 "q-79626" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529122 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529122 1071 "Which of the following gives the area bounded by graph of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529122 1072 #uuid "529c609b-d364-5c28-a5a9-2026822f240a" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529122 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529076 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529123 1069 "q-106057" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529123 1071 "Which of the following shows the graph of $y=2{x}^{2}+4x-3?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529123 1072 #uuid "53ebdab8-abca-5063-be77-653bbbd15d1e" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529123 1074 "Let's construct a table of values for the function $y=2{x}^{2}+4x-3.$ We fill the first row with the $x$-values $x=-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,$ and then use the formula $y=2{x}^{2}+4x-3$ to compute the corresponding $y$-values in the second row.\n\n| $\\,\\,x\\,\\,$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\,y\\,\\,$ | $3$ | $-3$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $13$ |\n\nIf we plot those points on a graph, we can start to see the shape of a parabola:\n\n![](images/b4/b43e0f6b6f359b08dd346a30509d82482a3b82defac90728ddaa7c9769690846.png)\n\nWe fill out the graph by drawing the parabola through the plotted points:\n\n![](images/8d/8de7d3c268e35f85da8f1bf7882d2ebd285fa3f5c0c239c986eeb1564277e4e1.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528953 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529124 1069 "q-350607" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529124 1071 "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529124 1072 #uuid "54f15411-79a5-546c-8ee4-965cb1e88cd5" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529124 1074 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $4.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{4},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}=\\frac{16}{81} \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5}=\\frac{32}{243}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}}=\\frac{(\\frac{32}{243})}{(\\frac{16}{81})}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{81})}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{81})}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529054 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529125 1069 "q-350582" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529125 1071 "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529125 1072 #uuid "56e0e8e1-0cfd-5d25-b2ed-327a18d60529" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529125 1074 "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{1}=3.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{2}}{8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-\\frac{3}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(\\frac{5}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529059 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529126 1069 "q-106054" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529126 1071 "Which of the following shows the graph of $y=-{x}^{2}+10x-28?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529126 1072 #uuid "57d98e6f-6b53-5ed4-abd4-c02ceb5b88f3" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529126 1074 "Let's construct a table of values for the function $y=-{x}^{2}+10x-28.$ We fill the first row with the $x$-values $x=3,4,5,6,7,8,$ and then use the formula $y=-{x}^{2}+10x-28$ to compute the corresponding $y$-values in the second row.\n\n| $\\,\\,x\\,\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\,y\\,\\,$ | $-7$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-7$ | $-12$ |\n\nIf we plot those points on a graph, we can start to see the shape of a parabola:\n\n![](images/b7/b762d214dbd72555b069846ca8e7bcb5248c73c15db10f06018167ff7966d6d0.png)\n\nWe fill out the graph by drawing the parabola through the plotted points:\n\n![](images/24/24aae5c27fc48b7a340e36f6a54d9d6d636644c938e5328c1a8c68713179a947.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529074 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529127 1069 "q-106029" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529127 1071 "![](images/68/687ea4a6fac8c4c6b7df7336f13b8ca39eb1b7049990c9875eeeaf556bd5353c.png)\n\nFor the parabola shown above, find the vertex and the equation of the axis of symmetry." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529127 1072 #uuid "5a174f7c-09d0-50e5-afac-66bf0cc4c315" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529127 1074 "The vertex is the \"turning point\" of the parabola. In an upwards parabola, like the one here, the vertex is the maximum point. So, the vertex of this parabola is $(5,6).$\n\nThe axis of symmetry is the vertical line that passes through the vertex. Therefore, the axis of symmetry of this parabola is $x=5.$\n\n![](images/ed/ed00e2896127e6f3da7fc8e63c22e1944434060ac53e4e63d31f1cd7590a8627.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529005 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529036 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529051 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529128 1069 "q-183313" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529128 1071 "$\\,$ $n<0\\text{ or }m>-5.$\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529128 1072 #uuid "5a6263d9-7a39-5199-9f93-7ac1161cd5e5" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529128 1074 "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$n<0\\text{ or }m>-5$\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(n<0\\text{ or }m>-5) & ≡¬(n<0∨m>-5) \\\\  & ≡¬(n<0)∧¬(m>-5) \\\\  & ≡n\\ge 0∧m\\le -5 \\\\  & ≡n\\ge 0\\text{ and }m\\le -5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528940 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529129 1069 "q-355832" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529129 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.670 & 0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ 0.224 & -0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224 \\\\ -0.670 & -0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ -0.224 & 0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.191 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.095 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529129 1072 #uuid "5bab4cd0-ae02-5029-8d61-df09bcee19e2" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529129 1074 "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents the projection of only the first datapoint (the first row of $X$) onto the first principal component. Therefore, it produces a single scalar value rather than the full one-dimensional dataset.\n\n- Option II corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of the full dataset $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\n- Option III represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529009 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529130 1069 "q-350579" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529130 1071 "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529130 1072 #uuid "5c0c2f91-9457-576f-ab5b-77c8f4a530ad" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529130 1074 "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}{4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{12}{5})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{12}{5})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529008 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529131 1069 "q-82042" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529131 1071 "![](images/12/12b20f973eee2011e128c27fcf529570a2fa28c892765206ca12395cd3b3774d.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529131 1072 #uuid "613bc0f6-6251-58fe-b528-1093bbbb500f" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529131 1074 "To graph the function $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed813305ba5087c0b70c6756c2a0b3c7c1957fad1a902e7b1c2540d4f727f3b6.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529010 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529132 1069 "q-362200" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529132 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=x+6$ and $y={x}^{2}.$ Find the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529132 1072 #uuid "61a0b761-b748-5011-958d-51f405f814ac" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529132 1074 "Let $f(x)=x+6$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ x+6 & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=3.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/dd/dd8dc332f56d3b851647e5ed0fa8208eb5d3daf6e704a42ab5cffdb78ed78585.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=x+6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{3}((x+6)^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{3}({x}^{2}+12x+36-{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+6{x}^{2}+36x-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})\\,{|}_{-2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{27}{3}+54+108-\\frac{243}{5})-(-\\frac{8}{3}+24-72+\\frac{32}{5})] \\\\  & =\\frac{500\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528963 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529133 1069 "q-352326" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529133 1071 "![](images/7a/7ab46adacda52da11a07c783905161c6a6c8ebadb77423d7c70acdb17321a50e.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529133 1072 #uuid "63188c95-9ec2-54ce-9302-58600c7d5645" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529133 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+5{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+\\frac{5(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}{2}+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{13}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{13}{2}\\theta -\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{13\\pi }{4}+6) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{13\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi +24}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529055 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529134 1069 "q-44066" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529134 1071 "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n\nII. $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n\nIII. $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529134 1072 #uuid "63be8186-8026-5f56-8153-6a18a81d6ce3" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529134 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)}{3}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528981 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529135 1069 "q-9641" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529135 1071 "![](images/ed/ed702f9d982d599fc0f175dc7d7dd022aeefc3f5463aade2a9db71313faf4866.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ as shown above." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529135 1072 #uuid "692ede8e-6241-512f-89fd-e5f23e8be1b4" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529135 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi })) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(\\pi -0)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0+\\frac{1}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528983 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529136 1069 "q-355836" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529136 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.583 & -0.434 & 0.687 & 0.016 \\\\ 0.49 & -0.561 & 0.046 & 0.666 \\\\ 0.506 & 0.466 & 0.725 & -0.029 \\\\ -0.405 & 0.529 & -0.027 & 0.746\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}1.917 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.528 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.709 & 0.706 \\\\ -0.706 & 0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.583 \\\\ 0.49 \\\\ 0.506 \\\\ -0.405\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 1.917$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529136 1072 #uuid "6b5fc782-a821-5ef2-823c-11a0d288b2b3" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529136 1074 "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Option II represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529048 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529137 1069 "q-43906" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529137 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529137 1072 #uuid "6e2c74ef-5be6-50fe-9eda-c944716ab881" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529137 1074 "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (-1))+2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+3+18} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528992 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529138 1069 "q-146353" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529138 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2+y-x}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-2x}{6}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1].$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529138 1072 #uuid "72ca9ca6-7469-59a8-9b29-546c35450d5e" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529138 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2+y-x}{3})}{(\\frac{5-2x}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2+y-x)}{5-2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{2(2+y-1)}{5-2\\cdot 1}=\\frac{2(1+y)}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{2(1+y)}{3}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528960 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529139 1069 "q-142472" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529139 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\nI. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n\nIII. $A$ has $4$ pivot columns" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529139 1072 #uuid "74f4be02-4fe9-5741-a4d1-7268f77a2f5f" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529139 1074 "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=3$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, not $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529042 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529140 1069 "q-78591" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529140 1071 "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529140 1072 #uuid "75a391e4-92f0-5306-8519-d804235fa403" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529140 1074 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{16}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{64}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{64}{125})}{(\\frac{16}{25})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{25})}{(1-\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{25})}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529025 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529141 1069 "q-79524" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529141 1071 "What is $\\frac{{a}^{2}-4a+4}{{a}^{2}-4}$ reduced to lowest terms?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529141 1072 #uuid "77fb920e-43ca-5282-8a0a-596d7af0b021" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529141 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator, canceling out any common factors that appear in both.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}^{2}-4a+4}{{a}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{(a-2)^{2}}{(a-2)(a+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(a-2)\\cancel{(a-2)}}{\\cancel{(a-2)}(a+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{a-2}{a+2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528998 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529142 1069 "q-350624" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529142 1071 "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(0.5)^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529142 1072 #uuid "7a6fcb51-44e7-59f8-9078-8afece3073a1" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529142 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=6(0.5)^{2(1)}=6(0.5)^{2}=6(0.25)=1.5,\n$$\n\nand the second term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=6(0.5)^{2(2)}=6(0.5)^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6(0.5)^{4}}{6(0.5)^{2}}=(0.5)^{2}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(0.5)^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{1-0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{0.75} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528985 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529143 1069 "q-97423" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529143 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=3t-2{t}^{2}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529143 1072 #uuid "7e56a686-12c8-5112-a821-3890332df74f" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529143 1074 "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=3t-2{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(0)=0,\\,p(1)=1,\\,p(2)=-2.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(0)p(0)+p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(0)]^{2}+[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529047 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529144 1069 "q-78588" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529144 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{n}=$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529144 1072 #uuid "7e8589d7-77a6-5ea2-bd7d-7f7a24b35382" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529144 1074 "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{1}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{1}}=0.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529056 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529145 1069 "q-23089" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529145 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the function $y=(x-2)^{2}+2?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529145 1072 #uuid "82cee2a3-9424-531b-bce3-49f1ee87bedc" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529145 1074 "Remember that the graph of $y=(x-h)^{2}+k$ is a translation of the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of $y=(x-h)^{2}+k$ is located at $(h,k).$\n\nHere, the parabola is translated $2$ units *right*, so we have $h=2.$ The parabola is also translated $2$ units *up*, so we have $k=2.$ \n\n![](images/4e/4e43c68bd7ab2da6fb0be21ecc69ab29e111c442219caaae70b819e9df3488be.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(2,2),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529060 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529146 1069 "q-108147" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529146 1071 "![](images/e5/e59a35140e008485fc092f716c318ff1ade67b68a8b9988150308af576759466.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above and the point $(0,3)$ lies on the curve. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)-1?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529146 1072 #uuid "856534cf-3669-5864-adb1-aa4947ae5494" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529146 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(0,3)$ as shown in the diagram. \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)-1$ can be obtained from $y=f(x)$ by translating it by $1$ unit *down*. So, it must pass through the point $(0,2).$\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/56/56c48b2115d50b1d4e6ab105ba8214275a43efe0267857370976df55a205252a.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528979 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529147 1069 "q-348315" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529147 1071 "![](images/f3/f33fde62793530cfe8970a9fea690fc71036312483ea617088a8d0a663d5133a.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529147 1072 #uuid "858e5587-42fe-5f2f-94b7-94758f1a2164" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529147 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\theta +\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529071 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529148 1069 "q-352298" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529148 1071 "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529148 1072 #uuid "85d4b81f-f257-5ee9-9249-f54ff8b0fda6" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529148 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0)+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =2\\pi +0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529017 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529149 1069 "q-352297" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529149 1071 "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529149 1072 #uuid "86ea583e-6e3e-5bb4-93ab-14ccfe18681c" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529149 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi +0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528950 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529150 1069 "q-86118" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529150 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=2-x$ and $y=4-{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529150 1072 #uuid "89b9341d-d36f-5db4-a196-956e022f6dd9" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529150 1074 "Let $f(x)=2-x$ and $g(x)=4-{x}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2-x & =4-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-1$ to $x=2.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/ce/ce74cc02284807691e00fc59d00240520d392cdc01b8efafcd66518d717a607e.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=2-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=4-{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[4-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[2-x]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}+4x+12)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529039 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529151 1069 "q-106039" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529151 1071 "![](images/9e/9e207974b8c7b872864cce6e3ef80335ed0c9d20ec1b10960d36fb6a86971878.png)\n\nFind the roots of the parabola shown above." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529151 1072 #uuid "8a08fd8f-f28b-522d-892c-2595551ed22c" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529151 1074 "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve does not intercept the $x$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the parabola does not have any roots." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528948 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529152 1069 "q-122004" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529152 1071 "![](images/9c/9c5d2e2426a817b62f8cd834d7d31786c5f2dfd52a7b784fd0bf337abed6c4fc.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $y={e}^{2-x}$ and $y={e}^{{x}^{2}},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529152 1072 #uuid "8f211adc-046d-5c01-ba2b-26f9e0e2be96" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529152 1074 "Let $f(x)={e}^{2-x}$ and $g(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {e}^{2-x} & ={e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2-x & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+x-2 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/8a/8a7f49165698bdac791a31a058c3228e6e2beab9f5ee9393971aa0e9aecd27b8.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)={e}^{2-x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}({[{e}^{2-x}]}^{2}-{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}({e}^{4-2x}-{e}^{2{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528974 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529153 1069 "q-106003" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529153 1071 "What are the coordinates of the minimum point of the function $y=(x+2)^{2}+5?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529153 1072 #uuid "9521123b-d716-51bb-900f-b70ee495c687" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529153 1074 "Remember that the graph of $y=(x-h)^{2}+k$ is a translation of the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of $y=(x-h)^{2}+k$ is located at $(h,k).$\n\nHere, the parabola is translated $2$ units *left*, so we have $h=-2.$ The parabola is also translated $5$ units *up*, so we have $k=5.$ \n\n![](images/8e/8e69ca48e852176d2ed258f062995835889374975492873fe0863ea782d87ebd.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(-2,5),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529006 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529154 1069 "q-29824" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529154 1071 "What is $\\frac{{z}^{2}-6z+9}{3-z}$ reduced to lowest terms?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529154 1072 #uuid "97b7ba7e-3c98-5de8-abc1-d6b6edb5c45e" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529154 1074 "The numerator can be factored as\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-6z+9=(z-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nCanceling out any common factors that appear in the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}-6z+9}{3-z} & = \\\\ -\\frac{{z}^{2}-6z+9}{z-3} & = \\\\ -\\frac{(z-3)^{2}}{z-3} & = \\\\ -\\frac{\\cancel{(z-3)}(z-3)}{\\cancel{z-3}} & = \\\\ -\\frac{z-3}{1} & = \\\\ 3-z. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529040 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529063 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529155 1069 "q-355903" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529155 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.46 & -0.2 & 0.07 & -0.88 \\\\ -1.4 & 0.15 & 0.73 & -0.87 \\\\ -0.24 & 1.53 & 0.68 & 1.35 \\\\ -0.15 & -1.25 & 0.23 & 0.73 \\\\ 1.32 & -0.23 & -1.72 & -0.32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.186 & 0.257 & 0.252 & -0.792 & -0.456 \\\\ 0.398 & 0.593 & 0.3 & 0.45 & -0.443 \\\\ 0.529 & -0.708 & 0.123 & -0.057 & -0.447 \\\\ -0.015 & 0.115 & -0.887 & 0.015 & -0.446 \\\\ -0.725 & -0.259 & 0.208 & 0.408 & -0.443\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.878 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.152 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.636 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.652 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & -0.419 & 0.045 & -0.68 \\\\ 0.38 & -0.525 & 0.761 & 0.04 \\\\ 0.654 & 0.205 & -0.148 & -0.713 \\\\ 0.262 & -0.712 & -0.63 & 0.166\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529155 1072 #uuid "9ccff77b-7d01-5bd7-aaf4-9fba844066ae" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529155 1074 "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.878,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2.152,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.636,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.652.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈51.7%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈80.6%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈97.3%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $51.7%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $80.6%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $97.3%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/cc/ccc57a7e978a8b123dd21da151c2b0fec3080be9d39f1c8f26cc3b19c0cbd2b1.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529031 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529156 1069 "q-11094" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529156 1071 "![](images/2b/2bf88537e9a37404dd10dd99769f6933879d07c1ffbb913adc2a4f52b11f48d6.png)\n\nConsider the finite region above bounded by $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529156 1072 #uuid "a5df2e25-639b-5613-82c1-85dbfc89566d" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529156 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)={x}^{3}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{x}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}(1-{x}^{5/2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/0c/0cdcf559f1498045d3d8cd725593da6adb0459addb1df7cc3c4167b2fd9b6214.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={x}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}-{[{x}^{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{7})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529073 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529157 1069 "q-140268" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529157 1071 "Let $V=C[0,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\pi ]\\,,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=k$ and $y(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529157 1072 #uuid "ab0bc45b-46cc-5674-97dd-e2aa819a42e4" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529157 1074 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }k(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(k\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+{k}^{2}t){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+{k}^{2}\\pi -k\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-0 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}\\pi  & =0 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529080 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529158 1069 "q-50227" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529158 1071 "![](images/37/37bf4ff4ad6eb63c9bf51bc5fd188ddc0f5bd58bfd4a097617f56fb5de151300.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=x$ where $x\\ge 0,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529158 1072 #uuid "abb34aa3-eece-5767-8c0d-162486a46e00" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529158 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $g(x)=x.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {x}^{3} & =x \\\\ {x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 1.$ But, since $x\\ge 0,$ the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f8/f8850008be348bd78f48837def8691d3a4155e70d13909369c70da2eb31fffb4.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=f(x)={x}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=g(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[x]}^{2}-{[{x}^{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{6})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529020 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529159 1069 "q-350577" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529159 1071 "Given that ${a}_{1}=5$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.2$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529159 1072 #uuid "ac1cfe40-911c-5173-ab2c-22ea711c204a" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529159 1074 "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=5.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.2.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(1-(-0.2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(1+0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529075 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529160 1069 "q-352321" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529160 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529160 1071 "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529160 1072 #uuid "acc4627b-a75b-5710-8130-818c19bd3eef" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529160 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(9+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(9+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +8(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(17\\theta +24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(17\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+12(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}))+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pi }{3}+12(\\sqrt{3})+2(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pi }{3}+14\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528975 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528980 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529069 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529161 1069 "q-183293" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529161 1071 "$\\,$ $a$ is odd and $b$ is odd.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529161 1072 #uuid "af2842e6-dea4-50ab-b263-09b224915c23" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529161 1074 "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$a$ is odd and $b$ is odd\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(a\\text{ is odd and }b\\text{ is odd}) & ≡¬(a\\text{ is odd}∧b\\text{ is odd}) \\\\  & ≡¬(a\\text{ is odd})∨¬(b\\text{ is odd}) \\\\  & ≡a\\text{ is even}∨b\\text{ is even} \\\\  & ≡a\\text{ is even or }b\\text{ is even}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529000 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529162 1069 "q-140437" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529162 1071 "Let $V=C[1,e]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[1,e],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{e}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=2$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2e-2$\n\nII. $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2$\n\nIII. $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529162 1072 #uuid "b0a4298c-3fe3-56e6-a8b6-d32ef03d865a" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529162 1074 "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{e}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ {v}^{′}=2 & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t){|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}2t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t){|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2e-0)-2t{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =2e-(2e-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529078 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529163 1069 "q-27000" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529163 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-3,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+1.$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529163 1072 #uuid "b9eb1708-044c-5408-bdf8-03d4b09b76eb" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529163 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)+1$ by $1$ unit *down.* \n\nHence, if the point $(-3,1)$ lies on $y=f(x)+1$, then the point \n\n$$\n(-3,1-1)=(-3,0)\n$$\n\nlies on $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/2c/2c76b54e0dc12d52fbc6e41a601d5e87a105537e055a9d4cee4c758b0dd3ae63.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(-3,1)$ lies on $y=f(x)+1$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3)+1 & =1 \\\\ f(-3) & =1-1 \\\\ f(-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $x=-3$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(-3,0)$ must lie on $y=f(x).$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528967 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529164 1069 "q-106037" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529164 1071 "![](images/dc/dc388ceccdd95990d69ebf1c2df36ab8ed0c83228d7684b13a7a791e9705851f.png)\n\nFind the roots of the parabola shown above." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529164 1072 #uuid "ba657a54-9357-5409-90cb-ee552f02dde2" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529164 1074 "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-5$ and $x=0.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529037 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529165 1069 "q-86145" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529165 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $x={y}^{2}-6$ and $x=-2.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529165 1072 #uuid "bb5bb053-af9b-561d-bd9f-9db0ee360eaf" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529165 1074 "Let $f(y)={y}^{2}-6$ and $g(y)=-2.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{2}-6 & =-2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-2$ and $y=2.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/ba/ba9886ace429eee313ac208f7a8aebd93993c734e4382656dcf31ef15d04cd00.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)={y}^{2}-6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}({[{y}^{2}-6]}^{2}-{[-2]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}({y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+32)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-4{y}^{3}+32y)|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{32}{5}-32+64)-(-\\frac{32}{5}+32-64)] \\\\  & =\\frac{384\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529067 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529166 1069 "q-146256" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529166 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(x+y)}{4}, & 0<x\\le 2y,\\,\\,0<y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}, & 0<y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1].$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529166 1072 #uuid "c0901d8a-5ab9-5481-aeea-fb9811603032" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529166 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3(x+y)}{4})}{3{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+y)}{4{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<x\\le 2y,\\,0<y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$ \n\nNow, substituting $y=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{(x+1)}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{(x+1)}{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{8}{3}+2)-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529001 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529167 1069 "q-108217" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529167 1071 "![](images/2f/2f877bbec2e880c8a64979997cb8694390f568ee7d2ad240d3a38f07de04157d.png)\n\nThe graph of $g(x)=f(x)+2$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529167 1072 #uuid "c103a5cd-d8c5-5183-99b4-f60981d83dcb" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529167 1074 "From the diagram, the graph of $y=f(x)+2$ passes through the point $(0,-2).$\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)+2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by translating $2$ units *up*. \n\nSo, the graph $y=f(x)$ would be $2$ units *below* the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$\n\n![](images/34/34927fd5b2a6245822c4d39ad38e15449d18b068b57dc6f7d7468037b1e53a59.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528937 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529033 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529168 1069 "q-355842" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529168 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.42 & -0.33 & -0.4 \\\\ -0.94 & 1.07 & -0.08 \\\\ 1.11 & 0.12 & 1.71 \\\\ -0.79 & -1.51 & -0.9 \\\\ 1.04 & 0.66 & -0.32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.244 & 0.013 & 0.022 & -0.603 & 0.759 \\\\ -0.051 & -0.763 & 0.397 & 0.405 & 0.307 \\\\ 0.671 & 0.439 & 0.394 & 0.235 & 0.383 \\\\ -0.644 & 0.452 & 0.021 & 0.57 & 0.237 \\\\ 0.272 & -0.144 & -0.828 & 0.305 & 0.355\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.730 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.751 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.210 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.617 & 0.395 & -0.68 \\\\ 0.461 & -0.882 & -0.095 \\\\ 0.638 & 0.255 & 0.727\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $85%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529168 1072 #uuid "c6804c35-f8e1-5715-b170-24ee05ad3186" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529168 1074 "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.730,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.751,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.210.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.730}^{2}}{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}+{1.210}^{2}}≈62.2%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}}{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}+{1.210}^{2}}≈87.8%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $62.2%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $87.8%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/b2/b2f5cc8cae6fac8d24e20c045990d39d088a34df26402a7f1e5137070579de03.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $85%$ of the variance." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528971 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529169 1069 "q-113063" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529169 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{3t-15}{3{t}^{2}-30t+75}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529169 1072 #uuid "c85a34c5-6e9b-5e37-9be6-8bd5b320956b" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529169 1074 "Here, we take out a factor of $3$ from the numerator and from the denominator, and then use the polynomial factorization ${t}^{2}-10t+25=(t-5)^{2}.$ Canceling out any common factors that appear in both the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3t-15}{3{t}^{2}-30t+75} & =\\frac{3(t-5)}{3({t}^{2}-10t+25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(t-5)}{3(t-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(t-5)}{3(t-5)(t-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}\\cancel{(t-5)}}{\\cancel{3}\\cancel{(t-5)}(t-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529052 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529170 1069 "q-113084" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529170 1071 "$\\frac{5{h}^{2}-10h-15}{4{h}^{2}-4h-8}=$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529170 1072 #uuid "ca2d9c6a-ef62-5982-8134-5f8260bfee4a" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529170 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator, canceling out any common factors that appear in both.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{h}^{2}-10h-15}{4{h}^{2}-4h-8} & =\\frac{5({h}^{2}-2h-3)}{4({h}^{2}-h-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(h+1)(h-3)}{4(h-2)(h+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cancel{(h+1)}(h-3)}{4(h-2)\\cancel{(h+1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(h-3)}{4(h-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5h-15}{4h-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528943 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528957 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529004 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529171 1069 "q-183283" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529171 1071 "$\\,$ $x$  is even and $y$ is prime.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529171 1072 #uuid "cd5faa7d-a0da-5855-abab-cb9f9cd47a6c" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529171 1074 "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x$  is even and $y$ is prime\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\text{ is even and }y\\text{ is prime}) & ≡¬(x\\text{ is even}∧y\\text{ is prime}) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\text{ is even})∨¬(y\\text{ is prime}) \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is odd}∨y\\text{ is not prime} \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is odd or }y\\text{ is not prime}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528970 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529172 1069 "q-14337" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529172 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{4{d}^{2}-36}{2(d+3)}?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529172 1072 #uuid "cd81b926-79ef-579e-9c6d-82f75c5992d4" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529172 1074 "The numerator is a difference of squares. It can be factored as\n\n$$\n4{d}^{2}-36=4({d}^{2}-9)=4(d+3)(d-3).\n$$\n\nCanceling out any common factors that appear in the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{d}^{2}-36}{2(d+3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(d+3)(d-3)}{2(d+3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2(d+3)}(d-3)}{\\cancel{2(d+3)}} & = \\\\ 2(d-3).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528958 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529173 1069 "q-79518" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529173 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{2x+10}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529173 1072 #uuid "cf71d35a-0b9f-5563-8501-c0ec218240c0" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529173 1074 "The numerator is a difference of squares. If we factor it and then cancel out any common factors that appear in both the numerator and the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{2x+10} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)(x+5)}{2(x+5)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)\\cancel{(x+5)}}{2\\cancel{(x+5)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{x-5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528955 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528959 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528995 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529021 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529022 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529035 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529045 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529065 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529070 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1069 "q-355960" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-2.506 & 4.309 & 6.738 \\\\ 0.552 & 4.174 & 1.558 \\\\ 0.175 & 3.635 & 2.487 \\\\ 1.127 & 4.541 & 5.878 \\\\ 4.088 & 4.508 & 5.688\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning that walks through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is \n{{field-1}} \nwith the \n{{field-2}} \nvariance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction in feature space \n{{field-3}} \nto the first\n{{field-4}}. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to {{field-5}}.\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=[\\begin{aligned}-1.35 & 0.21 & 0.99 \\\\ -0.06 & -0.16 & -1.27 \\\\ -0.22 & -1.63 & -0.86 \\\\ 0.19 & 0.84 & 0.61 \\\\ 1.44 & 0.75 & 0.53\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.155 & -0.805 & -0.027 & 0.027 & 0.572 \\\\ -0.358 & 0.242 & 0.758 & 0.142 & 0.467 \\\\ -0.675 & 0.055 & -0.551 & 0.438 & 0.214 \\\\ 0.396 & -0.030 & 0.144 & 0.887 & -0.184 \\\\ 0.485 & 0.538 & -0.317 & -0.019 & 0.612\\end{aligned}], \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.635 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.034 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.940 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.279 & 0.899 & -0.337 \\\\ 0.716 & 0.039 & 0.697 \\\\ 0.640 & -0.435 & -0.633\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n{{field-6}}.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n{{field-7}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-8}}.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={{field-9}}.\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1072 #uuid "d455f69c-b6d0-5a22-baea-4655fa27f3c7" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529174 1074 "We will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is $\\text{the direction in feature space}$ with the $\\text{most}$ variance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction $\\text{orthogonal}$ to the first $\\text{principal component}.$\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to $\\text{standardize the data}.$\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=[\\begin{aligned}-1.35 & 0.21 & 0.99 \\\\ -0.06 & -0.16 & -1.27 \\\\ -0.22 & -1.63 & -0.86 \\\\ 0.19 & 0.84 & 0.61 \\\\ 1.44 & 0.75 & 0.53\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.155 & -0.805 & -0.027 & 0.027 & 0.572 \\\\ -0.358 & 0.242 & 0.758 & 0.142 & 0.467 \\\\ -0.675 & 0.055 & -0.551 & 0.438 & 0.214 \\\\ 0.396 & -0.030 & 0.144 & 0.887 & -0.184 \\\\ 0.485 & 0.538 & -0.317 & -0.019 & 0.612\\end{aligned}], \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.635 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.034 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.940 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.279 & 0.899 & -0.337 \\\\ 0.716 & 0.039 & 0.697 \\\\ 0.640 & -0.435 & -0.633\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{2.635}^{2}}{{2.635}^{2}+{2.034}^{2}+{0.940}^{2}}≈58.0%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{2.635}^{2}+{2.034}^{2}}{{2.635}^{2}+{2.034}^{2}+{0.940}^{2}}≈92.6%.\n$$\n\nThus, we have that \n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $58.0%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $92.6%$ of the variance.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions}$ , since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={X}_{\\text{std}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nsince we need to project the data onto the first two principal components, which are the first two columns of $V.$\n\n**Note:** In order to invert the projection and interpret any further analysis in terms of the original features, we need to know the means and standard deviations of the original dataset $X.$ Additionally, in case we have more data, we can use the means and standard deviations of the original dataset to map the new data into the original feature space." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529077 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529175 1069 "q-78592" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529175 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529175 1072 #uuid "d588b5a8-8a0c-5e4d-8e17-d8c93ee42445" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529175 1074 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{9}{25})=\\frac{3}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{27}{125})=\\frac{9}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{125})}{(\\frac{3}{25})}=\\frac{9}{125}\\cdot \\frac{25}{3}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{25})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{25})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529013 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529176 1069 "q-123365" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529176 1071 "![](images/55/55308256c72aaf122c04c0b7f3a987fccd72d2513dd4edb6cf0ea4ce857522e5.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=2x+2$ and $x=\\sqrt{y-2},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529176 1072 #uuid "d84428ea-0ba4-5227-96d6-43b34422e545" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529176 1074 "Writing our first function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x+2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)=\\frac{1}{2}y-1$ and $g(y)=\\sqrt{y-2}.$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}y-1 & =\\sqrt{y-2} \\\\ y-2 & =2\\sqrt{y-2} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(y-2) \\\\ (y-2)^{2}-4(y-2) & =0 \\\\ (y-2)((y-2)-4) & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(y-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=2$ to $y=6.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/8e/8e621715ded3c5ed527d2367785707939b1e3593707eaafe0b4c5e4509883e49.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)=\\frac{1}{2}y-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)=\\sqrt{y-2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}({[\\sqrt{y-2}]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(y-2-{[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528993 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529177 1069 "q-352336" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529177 1071 "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529177 1072 #uuid "d870d48d-9aa5-5918-85fa-03f4b0df3775" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529177 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4{\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{\\theta }^{3}}{3}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{3}}{3})-2(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528982 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529178 1069 "q-30782" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529178 1071 "![](images/05/05213dec3ea87b199cdd6c4bd73f5dedc3e19df69fed5ceef4a44dda60365b4b.png)\n\nFind the roots of the parabola shown above." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529178 1072 #uuid "d8dc19dd-4912-57fc-b7e0-84c553fa7c79" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529178 1074 "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve intercepts the $x$ axis at the points $(3,0)$ and $(6,0).$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=3$ and $x=6.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528986 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529179 1069 "q-79793" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529179 1071 "Which of the following gives the total area enclosed by the graph of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\,$ for $\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529179 1072 #uuid "d9bf3624-c382-5070-9fe1-d9a42b576ac0" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529179 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528990 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529180 1069 "q-124588" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529180 1071 "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n}.$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529180 1072 #uuid "dd479934-e321-5035-bf71-51bd7984a9a8" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529180 1074 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}=5 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}=\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{6})}{5}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528951 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529181 1069 "q-86111" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529181 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=8x$ and $y={x}^{4}.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529181 1072 #uuid "de3cc4c9-f0a7-5d21-89e4-4f5e7276aeec" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529181 1074 "Let $f(x)=8x$ and $g(x)={x}^{4}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 8x & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{4}-8x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{3}-8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/ac/ac3248f7ada22b91c42971a0a85692625321cafa82ced12215671435ab584d00.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={x}^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=8x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[8x]}^{2}-{[{x}^{4}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(64{x}^{2}-{x}^{8})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528952 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529182 1069 "q-124584" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529182 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529182 1072 #uuid "df45f3a3-f545-5c42-98d5-edf671e88e36" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529182 1074 "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}{10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529023 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529183 1069 "q-26451" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529183 1071 "![](images/66/66d9e6755720befa3887a3c0e65833f396ce7442ae04f00c3d82721cfc804947.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529183 1072 #uuid "dfd0113c-14f1-5d6a-ac3b-dca77d3d998d" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529183 1074 "To graph the function $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cfa455809238b0d89c1b496283f6a8be602fa481e98a5e1d8ac72c48346f3703.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529062 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529184 1069 "q-108382" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529184 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+4.$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529184 1072 #uuid "e09e1da8-e383-596f-bdbd-80147bb9fb19" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529184 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)+4$ by $4$ units *down.* \n\nHence, if the point $(-2,3)$ lies on $y=f(x)+4$, then the point \n\n$$\n(-2,3-4)=(-2,-1)\n$$\n\nlies on $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/5f/5f74a8d57dc1766912c4490ab156d0f7f2ed0bbc9b4dbb9e53ae46c48ceb8bb5.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(-2,3)$ lies on $y=f(x)+4$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2)+4 & =3 \\\\ f(-2) & =3-4 \\\\ f(-2) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $x=-2$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(-2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(-2,-1)$ must lie on $y=f(x).$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528938 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529185 1069 "q-113086" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529185 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{{b}^{2}-7b+12}{4{b}^{2}+16b-84}?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529185 1072 #uuid "e14ac645-6583-57f4-8d3d-7907bb61243a" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529185 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator, canceling out any common factors that appear in both.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}^{2}-7b+12}{4{b}^{2}+16b-84} & =\\frac{{b}^{2}-7b+12}{4({b}^{2}+4b-21)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(b-3)(b-4)}{4(b+7)(b-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(b-3)}(b-4)}{4(b+7)\\cancel{(b-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{b-4}{4(b+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{b-4}{4b+28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529046 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529186 1069 "q-81962" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529186 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(3,-3)$ lies on the curve $y=3f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529186 1072 #uuid "e1e8554d-3b9d-57c5-a60f-771660835934" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529186 1074 "The function $y=3f(x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nThe point $Q(3,q)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $P(3,-3)$ on $y=3f(x).$ Since we know that the $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $3$ times the $y$-coordinate of $Q,$ we can form the following equation:\n\n$$\n3q=-3\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get $q=-1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of $Q$ are $(3,-1).$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529066 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529187 1069 "q-183280" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529187 1071 "$\\,$ $x$ is not an even integer, and $x$ divides $50.$\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- $C(x):$ $x$ is an even integer\n\n- $D(x):$ $x$ divides $50$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529187 1072 #uuid "e78ca7a7-d4e4-5c94-b10c-7178217e2c2b" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529187 1074 "The sentence \n\n$\\,$ $x$ is not an even integer.\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $¬C.$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is expressed symbolically as \n\n$$\n¬C∧D.\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529018 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529188 1069 "q-23258" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529188 1071 "What are the coordinates of the minimum point of the function $y={x}^{2}+5?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529188 1072 #uuid "ead518c9-914d-5e94-b01b-fb4224373ff4" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529188 1074 "In general, the graph of $y={x}^{2}+k$ looks like the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ but translated $k$ units up. The vertex of $y={x}^{2}+k$ is located at $(0,k).$\n\nFor the given function $y={x}^{2}+5,$ we have $k=5.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ after being translated $5$ units *up*. \n\n![](images/a9/a975d014ad92c31850e41367a2b5e46e85ab18a8a7e63bee81cd0f9b6af02bc0.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(0,5),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528939 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529189 1069 "q-105999" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529189 1071 "![](images/60/60d4ffad53a01f24e99b838e057b73f270cc41771db7f0718bcf731909182908.png)\n\nIdentify the equation that generated the graph above." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529189 1072 #uuid "eae2df82-c842-5432-893d-0301f5c3e7e4" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529189 1074 "In general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^{2}$ looks like the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^{2}$ is located at $(h,0).$\n\nHere, the curve shown is the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ after being translated $8$ units *right*. The vertex is located at $(8,0).$\n\nTherefore, $h=8,$ and the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x-8)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528994 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529190 1069 "q-23288" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529190 1071 "What are the coordinates of the minimum point of the function $y={x}^{2}+3?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529190 1072 #uuid "ecfb5c66-7387-5472-a64e-2ed6bd44ab74" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529190 1074 "In general, the graph of $y={x}^{2}+k$ looks like the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ but translated $k$ units up. The vertex of $y={x}^{2}+k$ is located at $(0,k).$\n\nFor the given function $y={x}^{2}+3,$ we have $k=3.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ after being translated $3$ units *up*. \n\n![](images/b6/b66bc0862b1a16cd71e846624903adea3f98a1b6d867a6516ec08cdee970518c.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(0,3),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point." 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528956 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529191 1069 "q-141342" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529191 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis\n\nII. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nIII. All columns of $A$ are pivot columns" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529191 1072 #uuid "f1d7f6a4-6114-5671-a52c-7df48bee5e24" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529191 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately. \n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis.* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ we have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$ which, in turn, means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ must have a basis.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is **not** invertible.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, by the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ we must have that $A$ is **not** invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.* $\\,\\times $\n\nSince $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{n},$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529083 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529192 1069 "q-26133" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529192 1071 "![](images/17/17c126ec4e6f901d038d28ca52ce3dedfb79abcd630694cfb465a57720fa6a42.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)-2$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529192 1072 #uuid "f5dc650c-6521-56fb-b358-5191cc78caea" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529192 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x)-2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by translating $2$ units *down*. \n\nSo, the graph $y=f(x)$ would be $2$ units *above* the graph of $y=f(x)-2.$\n\n![](images/1f/1f44fbd262f38470c13ae55acc11601491dde2662909e1a312600086a78b7adb.png)" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529068 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529193 1069 "q-50225" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529193 1071 "![](images/9b/9b328ecd6017922e429f11c57aca0ff452e4dc6a1476b14a448c6c9edd6b6679.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $x=2-\\sqrt{y}$ and $y=1-{x}^{2}$  from $y=0$ to $y=1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the $y$-axis?" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529193 1072 #uuid "f666ceb4-bd3b-5b00-9ee5-dd4ca3fe5f6b" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529193 1074 "Writing our second function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)=2-\\sqrt{y}$ and $g(y)=\\sqrt{1-y}.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7d/7d92b9f69ab27a2620ae94bfb4937509fc9db6dafdec4384af5de33a4dfecd30.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=\\sqrt{1-y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=2-\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{1-y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{1-y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529058 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529194 1069 "q-67301" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529194 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}=$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529194 1072 #uuid "fbd76d75-8ee7-5bcd-acb2-1f5ae9aa3787" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529194 1074 "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}{8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(1-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528989 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528996 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529044 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529195 1069 "q-183306" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529195 1071 "$\\,$ $n$ is positive or $m$ is negative.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529195 1072 #uuid "fbeaf179-7e47-5b15-8eb5-f5fa351b4082" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529195 1074 "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$n$ is positive or $m$ is negative\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(n\\text{ is positive or }m\\text{ is negative }) & ≡¬(n\\text{ is positive}∨m\\text{ is negative }) \\\\  & ≡¬(n\\text{ is positive})∧¬(m\\text{ is negative }) \\\\  & ≡n\\text{ is not positive}∧m\\text{ is not negative} \\\\  & ≡n\\text{ is not positive and }m\\text{ is not negative}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529032 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529196 1069 "q-146344" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529196 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1+y-x, & 0\\le x\\le 2y,\\,\\,0<y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0<y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1].$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529196 1072 #uuid "febf3d13-3a80-5fb8-bb8d-fc4ac7755bf9" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529196 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+y-x)}{2y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$ \n\nNow, substituting $y=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{(2-x)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{(2-x)}{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(4-\\frac{8}{3})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529197 1062 #uuid "0a7d2e71-2df5-5616-a66c-1039ae6b7480" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529197 1063 #edb/ref 17592186272348 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529198 1058 #edb/ref 17592186529201 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529198 1062 #uuid "1181624e-c0cd-5f60-b961-039085d0722f" 13194139535797 17592186045417 true] [17592186529198 1063 #edb/ref 17592186529166 13194139535797 17592186045417 true]